/* Faces: Charis (SIL Global), Plus Jakarta Sans (Tokotype) and JetBrains
   Mono, SIL Open Font License 1.1; each licence ships beside its files.
   Embedded unmodified as base64 so no page requests a font. */
@font-face {
  font-family: "Charis";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 400;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2");
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "Charis";
  font-style: italic;
  font-weight: 400;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2");
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "Charis";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 700;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2");
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "Plus Jakarta Sans";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 400;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2");
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "Plus Jakarta Sans";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 600;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2");
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "Plus Jakarta Sans";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 700;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2");
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "JetBrains Mono";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 400;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2");
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "JetBrains Mono";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 700;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2");
}

/* The whole reset. Each declaration earns its place.

   ⛔ Not a normalising library. A study page is prose, a few figures and a
   table; the browser's own defaults are already right for almost all of it.

   ⭐ `scroll-padding`: nothing sticks to the top, but
   the narration player sticks to the bottom, so a passage or a control scrolled
   to by the keyboard stops above it rather than under it. */

* { box-sizing: border-box; }
[hidden] { display: none !important; }
html {
  -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%;
  scroll-behavior: smooth;
  scroll-padding-top: 1.5rem;
  scroll-padding-bottom: calc(var(--player-height) + 1rem);
}
button, select, summary, a { touch-action: manipulation; }

/* Every colour, measure, face, radius and duration the page uses, defined once.

   ⭐ THIS FILE IS THE SHARED VOCABULARY. Every other stylesheet paints with
   `var(--token)` and defines no colour of its own, so changing the page's look
   is editing this file. `test_palette` asserts that no other authored part
   contains a raw colour, and recomputes every contrast pair below.

   ⭐ THE LOOK IS A ROAD SIGN ON COOL SLATE: a route planner's palette (asphalt
   ground, lane-paint ink, warning-yellow accent) and BOTH themes on the slate
   scale.
   - ⛔ COOL NEUTRALS, NEVER WARM. Every ground and every ink is on the slate
     scale: blue is the strongest channel, red the weakest. `test_identity`
     bounds the warmth of every ground and ink.
   - ⛔ CRISP, NOT DIM. Body text is about 12:1 in both themes, and each quieter
     ink sits in a band around 8.3:1 and 5.6:1. ⛔ Text is never pure white on
     dark and never pure black on light.
   - ⛔ NO GREEN, EXCEPT AS A STATUS. `test_identity` reads the hue of every
     token and refuses a saturated green, both ways, outside the status tokens.
     *Done, read, settled* is still the slate ink itself: the road behind you is
     solid, the road ahead is dashed.
   - ⭐ STATUS COLOURS (the 2026-11 redesign, the user's decision): `--ok`,
     `--bad` and `--warn`, each with a `-soft` ground, mark a verdict and
     nothing else — a quiz answer right or wrong, a run that passed or failed,
     a caution. ⛔ A status colour is never the only signal: it always comes
     with an icon and a word, so the page reads the same without colour.
   - ⭐ ONE LIVE ACCENT, AND IT IS ALLOWED TO BE LOUD. A
     warning yellow `#ffc933` by night and a deep amber by day. It means *next*,
     *here* and *speaking* and nothing else, and it is a mark, a line, a ring or
     a word — never a ground under running text. ⛔ The accent's CHROMA has a
     FLOOR, and a washed-out accent is what the check catches.
   - ⭐ Sky blue is the interactive ink: the focus ring, a type name in code.
     ⛔ Structure stays neutral — the margin rule and every hairline are slate.

   ⛔ THE ROLE NAMES ARE THE BRIEF'S, THE TOKEN NAMES ARE KEPT. Other parts
   (and the execution client) paint with the names below, so a role is
   renamed only together with every reader of it. The mapping: ground
   `--bg`, raised `--surface`/`--panel`, ink `--fg`, ink-soft `--fg-soft`,
   ink-faint `--muted`, rule `--rule`, next `--accent`, sign `--sign`.

   ⛔ BOTH THEMES, EVERY COLOUR TOKEN, AND THE READER CHOOSES. Dark is keyed on
   the reader's system until they say otherwise: `[data-theme="light"]` opts out
   of it and `[data-theme="dark"]` opts in, and the page
   carries a control that writes one of them and remembers the choice
   (`theme.js`, the boot in `page.html`). A reader who has chosen nothing still
   follows the system. */

:root {
  color-scheme: light dark;

  /* The faces are vendored and embedded in the stylesheet (`pageassets.faces`).
     ⛔ No banned face in any fallback: the first name is always the one used. */
  --font-prose: "Charis", Georgia, serif;
  --font-ui: "Plus Jakarta Sans", Verdana, sans-serif;
  --font-mono: "JetBrains Mono", "DejaVu Sans Mono", monospace;

  /* ⛔ `80ch`, AND EVERY DIGIT OF IT IS COUNTED. ⚠️ Charis's `ch` is its zero,
     which is wider than an average letter of running prose, so what a cap buys
     has to be COUNTED on a laid-out line and never reasoned from the token.

     ⭐ Counted over a real corpus's 41 unit and section pages, the worst
     paragraph's longest line, in characters:

       cap    76ch   80ch   82ch   83ch   84ch
       worst   101    106    109    109    111

     ⭐ The pinned image lays about three more characters on a line this long
     than a host browser (the same `ch` is 2.8% wider there), so `80ch` puts
     that worst paragraph at about 109. ⛔ 110 is the bound
     (`tests/visual/test_reading_room.py`'s `LONGEST_LINE`): past it the eye
     stops finding the start of the next line. ⚠️ At 1280px the cap does not
     bite at all — the column is narrower than it and the line runs to 84. */
  --measure: 80ch;
  --rail: 17rem;
  /* The column beside the reading surface for a page's secondary block — a
     unit's outline, the index's explanation — where the window has room. */
  --aside: 16rem;
  /* ⛔ THE SHELL'S CEILING, AND ABOVE IT THE SHELL CENTRES. `chrome.css` gives
     the shell `max-width: var(--page-max)` AND a left inset of half whatever the
     window has past it, so under the ceiling the inset floors at nothing and the
     page fills the window, and above it the shell holds this width with EQUAL
     margins. A shell pinned left would leave a dead band down the right of a
     wide window, and an unbounded one grows the reading column without limit.

     ⛔ WHY 110rem (1760px), FROM BOTH SIDES. The shell holds the rail
     (17rem), three gutters and the aside (16rem), so the reading surface is the
     shell less 588px: 1172px here. ⭐ FROM BELOW — the surface is what a code
     block is scanned in; over a real corpus's 218 code blocks, at a 1012px
     surface (`100rem`) 20.2% need a horizontal scrollbar, at 1172px (`110rem`)
     8.7%, at 1252px (`115rem`) 4.6%, at 1332px (`120rem`) 3.2%. ⛔ The first
     160px buys 11.5 points and the next 160px buys 5.5: `110rem` is the knee.
     ⭐ FROM ABOVE — every pixel past the knee is open room right of a paragraph
     that is capped at `--measure` (880px). At `110rem` that room is 292px,
     about the one outline column the reader can see beside it; at `120rem` it
     is 452px, nearly two, which reads as too wide. */
  --page-max: 110rem;
  --gutter: 1.25rem;
  /* The top bar's height: the page starts below it and the rail sticks under it. */
  --topbar: 3.25rem;
  --player-height: 3.5rem;

  /* Radii by job, and nothing else is rounded. */
  --radius-tight: 2px;
  --radius-control: 4px;
  --radius-sign: 10px;
  /* ⭐ A card: the opening scenario, a note, a practice, the quiz, the hub's
     group cards. One radius and one shadow so every card is the same object. */
  --radius-card: 12px;

  /* Motion answers an action and nothing moves on load. */
  --dur-quick: 120ms;
  --dur-pop: 300ms;
  --dur-fill: 350ms;
  --ease: cubic-bezier(.2, .7, .2, 1);

  --bg: #f8fafc;
  --surface: #ffffff;
  --surface-2: #e2e8f0;
  --fg: #22304a;
  --fg-soft: #445366;
  --muted: #556477;
  --rule: #dbe3ec;
  --rule-strong: #64748b;

  --accent: #a64d07;
  --accent-soft: #fdeec9;
  --practice: #334155;
  --practice-soft: #e2e8f0;

  --sign: #92400e;
  --sign-ink: #fef3c7;
  --margin: #dbe3ec;
  --done: #334155;

  --code-bg: #e8eef5;
  --code-fg: #22304a;

  /* Syntax, as a pen case of inks. Prism's token classes map onto these in
     code-highlight.css, and the ORDER of the groups there is the mapping. */
  --tok-comment: #556477;
  --tok-string: #854d0e;
  --tok-keyword: #6d28d9;
  --tok-number: #0e7490;
  --tok-type: #0369a1;
  --tok-function: #4338ca;
  --tok-punct: #475569;

  --hl-bg: #fdeec9;
  --hl-bar: #a64d07;
  --hl-fg: #22304a;
  --hl-code: rgba(166, 77, 7, .14);

  --panel: #ffffff;
  --shadow: 0 -1px 0 #dbe3ec;
  --ok: #15803d;
  --ok-soft: #f0fdf4;
  --bad: #b91c1c;
  --bad-soft: #fef2f2;
  --warn: #b45309;
  --warn-soft: #fffbeb;
  --term-bg: #0f172a;
  --term-fg: #e2e8f0;
  --term-muted: #94a3b8;
  --card-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(15, 23, 42, .06);
  --scrim: rgba(15, 23, 42, .45);
  --focus: #0369a1;
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root:not([data-theme="light"]) {
    --bg: #0f172a;
    --surface: #1e293b;
    --surface-2: #273548;
    --fg: #d2dbe6;
    --fg-soft: #aab7c9;
    --muted: #8f9eb4;
    --rule: #27354a;
    --rule-strong: #6e7f96;

    --accent: #ffc933;
    --accent-soft: #3a2e14;
    --practice: #cbd5e1;
    --practice-soft: #1e293b;

    --sign: #ffc933;
    --sign-ink: #0f172a;
    --margin: #27354a;
    --done: #cbd5e1;

    --code-bg: #0b1120;
    --code-fg: #cbd5e1;

    --tok-comment: #8296b0;
    --tok-string: #fcd34d;
    --tok-keyword: #c4b5fd;
    --tok-number: #67e8f9;
    --tok-type: #7dd3fc;
    --tok-function: #a5b4fc;
    --tok-punct: #94a3b8;

    --hl-bg: #3a2e14;
    --hl-bar: #ffc933;
    --hl-fg: #f1f5f9;
    --hl-code: rgba(255, 201, 51, .12);

    --panel: #1e293b;
    --shadow: 0 -1px 0 #27354a;
    --ok: #4ade80;
    --ok-soft: #052e16;
    --bad: #f87171;
    --bad-soft: #2a1215;
    --warn: #fbbf24;
    --warn-soft: #2b2110;
    --term-bg: #020617;
    --term-fg: #cbd5e1;
    --term-muted: #8796ab;
    --card-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(2, 6, 23, .5);
    --scrim: rgba(2, 6, 23, .7);
    --focus: #38bdf8;
  }
}

/* ⛔ The same values again, for a page that asks for dark whatever the system
   says. `test_palette` holds the two dark blocks equal. */
:root[data-theme="dark"] {
  color-scheme: dark;
  --bg: #0f172a;
  --surface: #1e293b;
  --surface-2: #273548;
  --fg: #d2dbe6;
  --fg-soft: #aab7c9;
  --muted: #8f9eb4;
  --rule: #27354a;
  --rule-strong: #6e7f96;
  --accent: #ffc933;
  --accent-soft: #3a2e14;
  --practice: #cbd5e1;
  --practice-soft: #1e293b;
  --sign: #ffc933;
  --sign-ink: #0f172a;
  --margin: #27354a;
  --done: #cbd5e1;
  --code-bg: #0b1120;
  --code-fg: #cbd5e1;
  --tok-comment: #8296b0;
  --tok-string: #fcd34d;
  --tok-keyword: #c4b5fd;
  --tok-number: #67e8f9;
  --tok-type: #7dd3fc;
  --tok-function: #a5b4fc;
  --tok-punct: #94a3b8;
  --hl-bg: #3a2e14;
  --hl-bar: #ffc933;
  --hl-fg: #f1f5f9;
  --hl-code: rgba(255, 201, 51, .12);
  --panel: #1e293b;
  --shadow: 0 -1px 0 #27354a;
  --ok: #4ade80;
  --ok-soft: #052e16;
  --bad: #f87171;
  --bad-soft: #2a1215;
  --warn: #fbbf24;
  --warn-soft: #2b2110;
  --term-bg: #020617;
  --term-fg: #cbd5e1;
  --term-muted: #8796ab;
  --card-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(2, 6, 23, .5);
  --scrim: rgba(2, 6, 23, .7);
  --focus: #38bdf8;
}

:root[data-theme="light"] { color-scheme: light; }

/* The focus ring, defined once, in the accent of whichever theme is showing.

   ⛔ `:focus-visible`, never `:focus`: the ring follows the keyboard and stays
   out of the mouse's way. And it is never removed — the reading floor is a page
   navigated by keyboard as readily as by pointer. */

:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--focus); outline-offset: 2px; border-radius: var(--radius-tight); }

/* The reading column: one rule for every block type the archive vocabulary
   names, and nothing about page chrome.

   ⭐ THE SCOPE IS THE BLOCK VOCABULARY, not one renderer's page. Eleven block
   types reach a page — heading, para, code, list, table, image, video, rule,
   quote, html and disclosure — and every one of them is styled here, so a
   renderer that emits a type nobody thought about renders as prose rather
   than as nothing. ⛔ Masthead, navigation rail, narration player, progress
   controls and practice panels are NOT here: they belong to the tasks that
   render them, and each adds its own part beside this one.

   ⭐ THE TYPE (the 2026-11 redesign): prose is Charis at 1.125rem on a
   1.72 line, headings are Plus Jakarta Sans at 700 with a slight negative
   track, on a scale with real jumps: 2.25 / 1.5 / 1.2rem. Sentence case is the
   corpus's to write; nothing here transforms case.

   ⭐ CARDS: the redesign lets a block that is not running text stand on a card
   — a quote or note, a disclosure, a code figure, a table. One radius
   (`--radius-card`), a hairline and `--card-shadow`; ⛔ never a coloured stripe
   down one side.

   ⚠️ Every selector here names a class this package publishes in
   `pageassets.surface`. A renderer that invents its own class names gets an
   unstyled page and no error, so the two are checked against each other. */

body {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0 var(--gutter);
  background: var(--bg);
  color: var(--fg);
  font: 400 1.125rem/1.72 var(--font-prose);
  text-rendering: optimizeLegibility;
}

/* --- prose ------------------------------------------------------------- */

h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 {
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-weight: 700;
  text-wrap: balance;
}
h1 { font-size: 2.25rem; line-height: 1.15; margin: 0 0 .6rem; letter-spacing: -.02em; }
h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { line-height: 1.3; margin: 2.4rem 0 .7rem; letter-spacing: -.01em; }
h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; }
h3 { font-size: 1.2rem; }
h4, h5, h6 { font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 600; }

p { margin: 0 0 1.1rem; }

/* ⛔ The reading's first block has no space of its own above it: the distance
   below the masthead is the masthead's to set, once. ⚠️ Without this the two
   shapes disagree by the first heading's own margin — in the wide shape `main`
   is a grid item, so that margin stays inside it, and in the narrow shape it
   collapses out through `main` and starts the reading lower. */
main > :first-child,
main > :first-child > :first-child { margin-top: 0; }

/* Running text alone keeps a measure the eye can track back from. A figure,
   a table or a code block is scanned rather than read and takes the full
   column. */
main p, main li { max-width: var(--measure); }

/* ⭐ Links in running text are ink with an underline: the page is written in
   one ink, and the underline (not a colour) is what says "link". The accent is
   kept for "next" and "you are here" (the plan's §4). */
a { color: inherit; text-decoration-color: var(--rule-strong); text-underline-offset: .18em; }
a:hover { text-decoration-color: currentColor; }

ul.items, ol.items { margin: 0 0 1.1rem; padding-left: 1.5rem; }
ul.items li, ol.items li { margin: .35rem 0; padding-left: .2rem; }

/* ⛔ CODE DRAWS NO LIGATURE. JetBrains Mono joins `!=` into one not-equal
   sign and `->` into an arrow, which reads as a different operator in a
   language where `!=` is two characters the reader must type. Every rule that
   sets the mono face turns them off, and the editor's `editor.fontLigatures`
   is off to match. */
code {
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  font-variant-ligatures: none;
  font-feature-settings: "liga" 0, "calt" 0;
  font-size: .82em;
  background: var(--code-bg);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
  padding: .05em .32em;
}

/* ⚠️ A long identifier in running text, a title or a list row
   (`SufficientAssumptionsForInfeasibility`) is one word with no place to break,
   and on a phone it pushed the whole page sideways. Inline code may break
   anywhere when nothing else fits; a listing keeps its lines whole and scrolls. */
:not(pre) > code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

/* A thematic break: one feint ruled line. */
hr {
  border: 0;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  margin: 2rem 0;
  max-width: var(--measure);
}

/* A quote holds arbitrary blocks, so it is a container and not a paragraph
   with italics. ⭐ In a course a quote is almost always a NOTE — what changes in
   a later version, a caution, an aside — so it stands on a card: a full hairline and the raised
   ground, ⛔ never a coloured stripe down one side. A note opened with a bold
   lead keeps that lead as its title. */
blockquote {
  margin: 1.8rem 0;
  padding: .9rem 1.15rem;
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  background: var(--surface);
  box-shadow: var(--card-shadow);
  color: var(--fg-soft);
  max-width: var(--measure);
}
blockquote strong:first-child { color: var(--fg); font-family: var(--font-ui); font-size: .95em; }
blockquote > :last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

/* --- present but withheld ---------------------------------------------- */

/* ⭐ A real `<details>`, which opens, closes and takes keyboard focus with
   scripting off entirely. ⛔ No card: a feint rule above and below, and the
   summary in the interface face. `open` is the author's, from the archive.
   ⛔ NO `max-width` OF ITS OWN. A disclosure is a CONTAINER, and a
   worked solution is a code figure inside one: capped at `--measure` it laid
   out narrower than every code figure in the flow and scrolled where they did
   not. Its running text is measured by `main p, main li` above, exactly as it
   is outside the disclosure. */
details.disclosure {
  margin: 1.6rem 0;
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  background: var(--surface);
}
/* ⚠️ The card's inset is on its children, not on the card, and a code figure
   inside takes none: a worked solution must lay out exactly as wide as a code
   figure in the flow (`test_reading_width`), the card's hairline included. */
details.disclosure > :not(figure) { margin-left: 1rem; margin-right: 1rem; }
details.disclosure > figure { margin-left: -1px; margin-right: -1px; border-radius: 0; }
details.disclosure > figure:last-child { border-radius: 0 0 var(--radius-card) var(--radius-card); margin-bottom: -1px; }
details.disclosure > summary {
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: .92rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  cursor: pointer;
  padding: .75rem 0;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
}
details.disclosure[open] > summary { color: var(--fg); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); margin-bottom: .9rem; }
details.disclosure > :last-child:not(figure) { margin-bottom: 1rem; }

/* --- figures ----------------------------------------------------------- */

figure { margin: 1.6rem 0; }

/* ⭐ Code is a different kind of text, so it keeps a ground of its own, on a
   card: a hairline, the card radius and a caption bar naming the file or the
   language, with the copy control at its right. */
figure.code {
  background: var(--code-bg);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  overflow: hidden;
}
figure.code figcaption {
  display: flex;
  gap: .75rem;
  align-items: center;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: .8rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--muted);
  background: var(--surface);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  padding: .4rem .6rem .4rem .9rem;
}
/* ⭐ A program's OUTPUT is not code the reader writes, so it is framed as a
   terminal, dark in both themes: a reader scanning the page never mistakes a
   log for source. `data-frame="terminal"` is set by the renderer for the
   output languages (`text`, `output`, `console`, `stdout`). */
figure.code[data-frame="terminal"] { background: var(--term-bg); border-color: var(--term-bg); }
figure.code[data-frame="terminal"] figcaption {
  background: var(--term-bg);
  color: var(--term-muted);
  border-bottom-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--term-muted) 25%, transparent);
}
figure.code[data-frame="terminal"] code { color: var(--term-fg); }
figure.code[data-frame="terminal"] button.copy { color: var(--term-muted); border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--term-muted) 45%, transparent); }
figure.code[data-frame="terminal"] button.copy:hover { color: var(--term-fg); border-color: var(--term-muted); }
figure.code figcaption .what { flex: 1 1 auto; }
/* A language the vendored highlighter does not cover renders plain, and the
   caption says so, so a plain block never looks like a broken highlighter. */
figure.code figcaption .unhighlighted { flex: 0 0 auto; font-style: italic; }

/* ⛔ THE SIZE AND THE LINE HEIGHT ARE THE `pre`'S, NOT THE `code`'S.
   A `pre` left at the body's 19px/1.65 lays every line on a 19px strut,
   so 13px code sat twice as far apart as it should — the inline `code`'s own
   line height cannot shrink the line box its block parent sets. */
figure.code pre {
  margin: 0;
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  font-variant-ligatures: none;
  font-feature-settings: "liga" 0, "calt" 0;
  padding: .85rem 1rem 1rem;
  overflow-x: auto;
  font-size: .82rem;
  line-height: 1.65;
}
figure.code code {
  background: none;
  border: 0;
  padding: 0;
  font-size: inherit;
  line-height: inherit;
  color: var(--code-fg);
}

button.copy {
  font: inherit;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--muted);
  background: none;
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: 6px;
  padding: .15rem .55rem;
  cursor: pointer;
}
button.copy:hover { color: var(--fg); border-color: var(--fg-soft); }

figure.image img { display: block; max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: var(--radius-tight); }
figure.image figcaption {
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: .85rem;
  color: var(--muted);
  margin-top: .5rem;
}

figure.video { margin: 1.6rem 0; }
/* ⛔ Capped so a video cannot dominate the reading column — and `70vh` is why
   `video-player.css` has to undo it in fullscreen, where `vh` resolves against
   the whole screen and would leave a black band across the bottom third. */
figure.video video {
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
  max-height: 70vh;
  display: block;
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
}

/* --- tables ------------------------------------------------------------ */

/* ⛔ The wrapper is not decoration. A table wider than the column must scroll
   inside its own box; without this the page itself scrolls sideways and every
   paragraph goes with it. ⚠️ It is positioned so that it also holds what is
   positioned inside it: KaTeX writes each formula's MathML copy absolutely
   placed, and a formula in a far column would otherwise widen the page. */
.scroll {
  position: relative;
  overflow-x: auto;
  margin: 0 0 1.6rem;
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  background: var(--surface);
}
table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: .92rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
th, td {
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  padding: .55rem .8rem;
  text-align: left;
  vertical-align: top;
}
th { font-family: var(--font-ui); font-size: .8rem; font-weight: 600; color: var(--muted); background: var(--bg); }
tr:last-child > td { border-bottom: 0; }

/* --- what the reader asked for ----------------------------------------- */

/* ⭐ Every motion on the page answers an action, and under this preference
   none of them runs: no transition, no animation, no smooth scroll. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  html { scroll-behavior: auto; }
  *, *::before, *::after { animation: none !important; transition: none !important; }
}

/* Callouts and the lesson's lead: two cards a lesson's prose stands on (the 2026-11 redesign).

   ⭐ A CALLOUT is a quote the author marked with a GitHub alert, `> [!WARNING]`
   (`archive.callouts`, `page.blocks.prose`): `blockquote[data-callout]`, the same card every
   quote stands on (`reading.css`), under a small label — an icon and the kind's word. ⛔ The
   kind never rests on colour alone: the word and the icon's shape say it in every theme.
   ⛔ A hairline and a subtle tint, NEVER a coloured stripe down one side.
   - note and tip stay on the neutral card: they are asides, not alarms.
   - important takes the accent on its label and a hint of it in its hairline. ⛔ Never the
     accent as a ground under running text (`palette.css`).
   - warning and caution take the status tint, `--warn-soft`, and `--warn` on the label.
   - scenario is the situation a unit opens on, labelled because the author asked for it.

   ⭐ The LEAD is the paragraph right under a unit's title (`p[data-lead]`, `page.section`):
   the same card as a scenario, with no label. Its words and its clip are the paragraph's own. */

blockquote[data-callout] > .eyebrow {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .45rem;
  margin: 0 0 .4rem;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: .74rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: .07em;
  line-height: 1.4;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
}
blockquote[data-callout] > .eyebrow svg { flex: 0 0 auto; }

blockquote[data-callout="important"] {
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 35%, var(--rule));
}
blockquote[data-callout="important"] > .eyebrow,
blockquote[data-callout="scenario"] > .eyebrow { color: var(--accent); }

blockquote[data-callout="warning"],
blockquote[data-callout="caution"] {
  background: var(--warn-soft);
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--warn) 35%, var(--rule));
}
blockquote[data-callout="warning"] > .eyebrow,
blockquote[data-callout="caution"] > .eyebrow { color: var(--warn); }

/* ⭐ The opening cards read as the lesson's own text, in its own ink, a little larger than a
   note's aside. */
blockquote[data-callout="scenario"],
p[data-lead] {
  color: var(--fg);
  font-size: 1.04em;
}
blockquote[data-callout="scenario"] { padding: 1.05rem 1.35rem 1.15rem; }

p[data-lead] {
  margin: 1.4rem 0 2rem;
  padding: 1.05rem 1.35rem 1.15rem;
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  background: var(--surface);
  box-shadow: var(--card-shadow);
}

@media (max-width: 40rem) {
  blockquote[data-callout="scenario"],
  p[data-lead] { padding: .9rem 1rem 1rem; font-size: 1em; }
}

/* The page's frame: the column, the margin rule, the masthead and trail, and
   the rail that reaches the other containers, at both widths.

   ⭐ ONE OF FOUR CHROME PARTS, SPLIT AT SEAMS BY JOB. This file
   was 748 lines against R11's 400 with no justification in it. It divides by
   what each part answers:
   - `chrome.css` (this): WHERE THINGS ARE — the column, the rail beside it or
     folded above it, the masthead and trail, the skip link, the margin rule.
   - `lists.css`: WHAT THE READER NAVIGATES AND WHERE THEY ARE IN IT — the
     outline, the unit and contents lists with their tick boxes, the index's
     progress, strip and filter.
   - `onward.css`: WHERE THE READER GOES NEXT — the between-pages bar and the
     Up next slip, the one bold element.
   - `notes.css`: WHAT THE PAGE TELLS THE READER AND WHAT IT OFFERS THEM — the
     index's explanation, the practices note, the read control and the theme
     control.

   ⛔ NOT ONE COLOUR AND NOT ONE CLASS in any of the four. Every value is a
   `palette.css` token and every region is reached by element, `aria-label` or
   a published `data-*` hook, so no class joins the markup contract.

   ⚠️ Loaded after `reading.css` and before `code-highlight.css`: two rules of
   equal specificity mean the last one wins, and the page column below refines
   what `reading.css` sets on `body`. */

/* --- the column ---------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ⛔ ONE BOUND, ON `body`, IN THE PROSE MEASURE. `ch` resolves against the
   element's own font, so a bound written on each region gave each region a
   different column. `body` carries the prose face the measure is chosen in.
   Four gutters leave the measure plus one gutter of air either side. This is
   the narrow shape; the wide shape at the foot of this file bounds the same
   element.

   ⭐ THE MARGIN RULE. A pale line down every
   page: left of it is the reader's (the course, their ticks, where the
   narration is), right of it is the work. It is drawn once, by one
   pseudo-element on `body`, with no markup. ⚠️ It is structure, never a
   signal — `--margin` sits below 3:1 on purpose, because nothing is told apart
   by it alone, and it is a neutral hairline, not a red one. The
   left padding gives the text room to its right. */

body {
  max-width: calc(var(--measure) + 4 * var(--gutter));
  margin-left: auto;
  margin-right: auto;
  position: relative;
  min-height: 100vh;
  padding-left: calc(var(--gutter) + .75rem);
  /* ⚠️ A title may hold a long identifier as plain text
     (`sufficient_assumptions_for_infeasibility`); on a phone it would push the
     page sideways. A word that cannot fit on a line breaks; nothing else moves. */
  overflow-wrap: break-word;
}

body::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  bottom: 0;
  left: .75rem;
  width: 2px;
  background: var(--margin);
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* --- the skip link --------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ⭐ The first focusable element on every page, out of sight until the
   keyboard reaches it (the brief's §3). `page.html` writes it; it is reached by
   what it is — a link to the content — and needs no hook of its own. */
body > a[href="#content"] {
  position: absolute;
  left: 1.5rem;
  top: -4rem;
  z-index: 2;
  padding: .4rem .8rem;
  background: var(--surface);
  color: var(--fg);
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
}
body > a[href="#content"]:focus-visible { top: .75rem; }

/* --- the masthead and the trail ------------------------------------------ */

/* ⭐ The trail sits ABOVE the title: where the page is comes
   before what it is called. Then the title, heavy; then one quiet line. No
   rule under it and no capitals: the brief's eyebrow tell is gone. */

body > header {
  padding: 2rem 0 1.25rem;
  margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
}

body > header h1 { margin: 0 0 .35rem; }

body > header p {
  margin: 0;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: .95rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

/* ⛔ One quiet line, the page it is on told apart by WEIGHT as well as colour.
   The `›` between crumbs is markup, so a page with no stylesheet still reads
   as a trail. ⚠️ A long first crumb (a corpus title) is cut with an ellipsis;
   its full text is still the link's text for assistive technology. */
nav[aria-label="Breadcrumb"] {
  margin: 0 0 .75rem;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: .9rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

nav[aria-label="Breadcrumb"] ol {
  display: flex;
  gap: .4rem;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
  white-space: nowrap;
}

/* The trail keeps to one line: the first crumb (the whole corpus's title)
   gives way with an ellipsis before the page's own name does. ⚠️ A middle
   crumb may be a whole sentence too, so on a phone every crumb gives way, none
   below a few letters, rather than the trail pushing the page sideways. */
nav[aria-label="Breadcrumb"] li { flex: 0 1 auto; min-width: 3rem; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; }
nav[aria-label="Breadcrumb"] li:first-child {
  flex: 0 1 auto;
  min-width: 3rem;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}
nav[aria-label="Breadcrumb"] li[aria-current="page"] { flex: 0 1 auto; min-width: 0; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; }

nav[aria-label="Breadcrumb"] a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }
nav[aria-label="Breadcrumb"] a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

nav[aria-label="Breadcrumb"] li[aria-current="page"] {
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--fg);
}

/* On a phone the trail's last crumb is the title printed right under it, so
   the line gives it up rather than cutting both it and the way back short. */
@media (max-width: 40rem) {
  nav[aria-label="Breadcrumb"] li[aria-current="page"] { display: none; }
}

/* ⛔ The corpus's level word (`group`, `module`) is not shown in the trail or
   the rail: indentation already says the depth, and a word that tells the
   reader nothing is the brief's "label that stops being true". It stays in the
   markup (R1: the corpus's own word), and the index's tree keeps it. */
nav[aria-label="Breadcrumb"] span[data-kind="level"],
nav[aria-label="Containers"] span[data-kind="level"] { display: none; }

/* --- the rail across containers, in the column --------------------------- */

/* ⛔ A plain list on the ground, not a card. At a narrow width it is folded
   into one line — `rail.html` wraps it in one `<details>`, which the page
   script closes below the threshold and leaves open with no script (the floor
   is today's open list). ⭐ Units are rows on one feint line, ordinals in a
   fixed column; the current unit has the accent's wash. ⚠️ The rail does not
   also mark the next unit: the Up next slip at the foot of the page names it,
   and a second marker here would be chrome competing with the one bold
   element. */

/* ⛔ A title with no break in it (`RecursiveTask/RecursiveAction`) wraps
   inside its row rather than running out of the column. */
nav[aria-label="Containers"] {
  margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: .86rem;
  line-height: 1.4;
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}

nav[aria-label="Containers"] > details > summary {
  padding: .4rem 0 .6rem;
  font-size: .8rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: .02em;
  color: var(--muted);
  cursor: pointer;
}

nav[aria-label="Containers"] ol {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

nav[aria-label="Containers"] ol ol {
  margin: .1rem 0 .6rem;
  padding: 0;
}

nav[aria-label="Containers"] li details > summary {
  padding: .45rem 0 .3rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--fg);
  cursor: pointer;
}

/* ⭐ The summary's link is inline, so the disclosure mark sits on the title's
   own line (R2: it stood alone above it and read as a stray glyph). */
nav[aria-label="Containers"] summary a { display: inline; color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }
nav[aria-label="Containers"] summary a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

nav[aria-label="Containers"] li li > a,
nav[aria-label="Containers"] li li[aria-current="page"],
nav[aria-label="Containers"] li li[data-readable="false"] {
  display: flex;
  position: relative;
  padding: .32rem 1.5rem .32rem .2rem;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
  text-decoration: none;
  border-radius: 6px;
}

nav[aria-label="Containers"] li li > a:hover { color: var(--fg); background: var(--surface-2); }

nav[aria-label="Containers"] span[data-kind="numbering"] {
  flex: 0 0 1.9rem;
  color: var(--muted);
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  font-variant-ligatures: none;
  font-feature-settings: "liga" 0, "calt" 0;
  font-size: .74rem;
  line-height: 1.75;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  text-align: right;
  padding-right: .55rem;
}

/* ⛔ The current container's mark reaches its OWN summary and never the rows
   nested under it. The current unit is told apart by weight AND by the wash;
   `aria-current` is the statement and this is the reinforcement. */
nav[aria-label="Containers"] li[aria-current="true"] > details > summary,
nav[aria-label="Containers"] li[aria-current="page"] {
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--fg);
}

/* ⭐ The 2026-11 redesign: the current unit is a soft wash with the accent
   ink, and no stripe down one side. */
nav[aria-label="Containers"] li li[aria-current="page"] {
  background: var(--accent-soft);
  color: var(--accent);
}
nav[aria-label="Containers"] li li[aria-current="page"] span[data-kind="numbering"] { color: var(--accent); }

/* ⛔ `data-readable="false"` is §7's third state — listed, not openable — and
   is legible without colour: italic, and no pointer affordance. */
nav[aria-label="Containers"] li[data-readable="false"] {
  font-style: italic;
  color: var(--muted);
}

/* ⭐ A unit the reader marked read ends its row with a tick, in the
   colour the listings' read box takes. ⚠️ `data-marked` is set at read time by
   `progress-view.js` and emitted by no renderer (R10). */
nav[aria-label="Containers"] li li[data-marked="true"] > a::after,
nav[aria-label="Containers"] li li[data-marked="true"]:not(:has(> a))::after {
  content: "\2713";
  /* ⚠️ Out of the flow, in room every row reserves: a tick that joined the
     row's text could wrap its title and move the row (`test_rail_marks`). */
  position: absolute;
  right: .5rem;
  top: .32rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--done);
}

/* ⭐ The words a read row says to assistive technology, in the rail
   and in both lists: kept in the accessibility tree and off the screen, so the
   tick above and the listings' box stay the only visual mark. ⚠️ `hidden` on
   an unread row still removes them; this rule sets no `display`. */
span[data-kind="read-state"] {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  margin: -1px;
  padding: 0;
  border: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  clip-path: inset(50%);
  white-space: nowrap;
}

/* --- the rail beside the column ------------------------------------------ */

/* ⛔ A left bar, flush to the window's edge, fixed while the page scrolls.
   ⭐ Three rules of CSS over the same bytes, asked for by the
   page that HAS the region (`:has()`), so the root index gets no empty track.
   The reading column is what is left of the viewport (`1fr`), and the prose
   inside it keeps `--measure` (`reading.css`).

   ⛔ The rail spans eleven rows because `page.html` declares eleven top-level
   positions inside `<body>`: a negative line would count from the end of the
   EXPLICIT grid, which has columns only. The unit mirror reads this number
   back against the skeleton. Sticky and not fixed, with `max-height: 100vh` and
   `overflow-y: auto` as one decision: a long course list scrolls itself.

   ⭐ The margin rule moves to the rail's right edge, between the reader's
   column and the work. `72rem` is the one threshold in these parts.

   ⭐ THE ASIDE: a page with a
   secondary block — a unit's outline, the index's explanation — gives it a
   third column, `--aside`, on the far side of the reading column, so a wide
   window's room holds it and the list or the prose starts at the top. ⛔ No
   second threshold: at `72rem` the prose column is a little narrower than
   `--measure` and reaches it as the window widens, and below `72rem` the block
   is back in the one column above the content. The block is the page's own
   aside: it is sticky and scrolls itself, as the rail does.

   ⛔ ONE SHAPE THAT WORKS ON EVERY DISPLAY. A shell pinned left leaves a dead
   band down the right of a wide window, and an unbounded one grows the reading
   column away from the prose inside it.

   ⭐ SO THE SHELL IS BOUNDED AND CENTRED, WHICH IS ONE SHAPE AND NOT TWO.
   `max-width: var(--page-max)` with `margin-inline: auto`: below the ceiling
   the margins resolve to nothing and the page fills the window, and above it the same declaration holds the shell at the
   ceiling with EQUAL margins either side. ⛔ There is no width at which one
   side has room the other does not — which is what a dead band IS — and the
   rail, the reading column and the outline are all inside the shell, so the
   reader's menu centres with the reading rather than clinging to the glass.

   ⛔ THE CEILING IS ON THE SHELL AND `> main` CARRIES NO BOUND: with the shell
   held at `--page-max` the surface can never reach it, so a second declaration
   of the same token would be a ceiling that never bites and a reader would have
   two places to look. ⭐ The token's argument — why `110rem` and not `100` or
   `120` — is in `palette.css` beside the declaration.

   ⚠️ THE INSET IS WRITTEN OUT RATHER THAN LEFT TO `auto`, AND THIS IS WHY.
   `margin-inline: auto` would centre the shell and say nothing a second rule
   could agree with — and the page with NO rail is bounded by the measure, not
   by the ceiling, so centring that one puts it somewhere else entirely and the
   layout jumps when a reader clicks between the two kinds. ⭐ Both shapes take
   the SAME left inset instead: half of whatever the window has past the
   ceiling, and nothing at all below it. ⛔ The two declarations are asserted
   EQUAL to each other in `test_chrome_page_start.py`, never against a value
   typed there, so they cannot drift apart. */

@media (min-width: 72rem) {
  body:has(nav[aria-label="Containers"]) {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: var(--rail) minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: var(--gutter);
    max-width: var(--page-max);
    margin-left: max(0px, (100% - var(--page-max)) / 2);
    padding-left: 0;
  }

  body:has(nav[aria-label="Containers"]):has(> nav[aria-label="Outline"], > section[aria-label="About this site"]) {
    grid-template-columns: var(--rail) minmax(0, 1fr) var(--aside);
  }

  body:has(nav[aria-label="Containers"])::before {
    left: calc(var(--rail) + var(--gutter) / 2 - 1px);
  }

  /* ⛔ The page without a rail starts where the page with one starts,
     keeping its own gutter. ⚠️ That place is the shell's
     left edge rather than the window's, so this is the same expression the
     shape above takes and the right margin is still left to `auto`. */
  body:not(:has(nav[aria-label="Containers"])) {
    margin-left: max(0px, (100% - var(--page-max)) / 2);
  }

  body:has(nav[aria-label="Containers"]) > * {
    grid-column: 2;
  }

  body > nav[aria-label="Containers"] {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 1 / span 11;
    align-self: start;
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    max-height: 100vh;
    overflow-y: auto;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1.5rem .5rem 2rem var(--gutter);
    border-left: 0;
    border-start-start-radius: 0;
    border-end-start-radius: 0;
  }

  body:has(nav[aria-label="Containers"]) > nav[aria-label="Outline"],
  body:has(nav[aria-label="Containers"]) > section[aria-label="About this site"] {
    grid-column: 3;
    grid-row: 1 / span 11;
    align-self: start;
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    max-height: 100vh;
    overflow-y: auto;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 2rem 0 2rem;
  }

  /* The fold is a narrow-width device: beside the column the list is always
     shown, and the page script reopens it if the window widens. */
  nav[aria-label="Containers"] > details > summary { display: none; }
}

/* ⭐ The unit's second line (the 2026-11 redesign, `page.unitmeta`): container,
   place, minutes, practices, questions. ⛔ The separator is DRAWN — a small
   circle — never a typed middle dot. */
body > header ul[data-section="unit-meta"] {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .25rem .7rem;
  margin: .2rem 0 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: .86rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}
body > header ul[data-section="unit-meta"] li { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .7rem; }
body > header ul[data-section="unit-meta"] li:first-child { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }
body > header ul[data-section="unit-meta"] li + li::before {
  content: "";
  width: 3px;
  height: 3px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: currentColor;
  opacity: .6;
}
/* ⚠️ Where the line wraps (a phone), a drawn dot would open the second line, so
   the parts are told apart by the gap alone. */
@media (max-width: 40rem) {
  body > header ul[data-section="unit-meta"] { gap: .25rem 1.1rem; }
  body > header ul[data-section="unit-meta"] li + li::before { display: none; }
}

/* What the reader navigates, and where they are in it.

   ⭐ ONE OF THE FOUR CHROME PARTS (`chrome.css` says how they divide).
   This one holds the in-page outline, the two lists that ARE their pages (a
   container's units, the root index's tree), the progress line and the
   segmented strip, and the filter. The Up next slip is `onward.css`.

   ⭐ HOW "WHERE YOU ARE" IS DRAWN, everywhere below, by FORM before colour:
   - a unit read: a SOLID ink tick box with a paper tick in it;
   - the unit up next: an EMPTY box OUTLINED in the accent, and the words
     "Up next" beside it;
   - every other unit: an empty ruled box.
   The boxes sit on one feint vertical line, like the tick column of a workbook.

   ⛔ No colour, no class, no second width threshold: see `chrome.css`. */

/* --- the in-page outline -------------------------------------------------- */

/* ⛔ Not a card (O1). A quiet list under the masthead, labelled "On this
   page" in sentence case (O2), unlined until hovered — it is navigation, not
   prose. */
nav[aria-label="Outline"] {
  margin: 0 0 .5rem;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: .84rem;
  line-height: 1.45;
}

nav[aria-label="Outline"] > p {
  margin: 0 0 .6rem;
  font-size: .78rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: .02em;
  color: var(--muted);
}

nav[aria-label="Outline"] ol {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

nav[aria-label="Outline"] li { margin: 0; }
nav[aria-label="Outline"] ol ol { margin: 0 0 0 .75rem; }
nav[aria-label="Outline"] a {
  display: block;
  padding: .3rem 0 .3rem .75rem;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
  text-decoration: none;
  border-left: 2px solid transparent;
}
nav[aria-label="Outline"] a:hover { color: var(--fg); }
/* ⭐ Scroll-spy (the 2026-11 redesign): `reading-progress.js` marks the link
   of the section being read with `aria-current="location"`, told apart by
   colour AND weight AND the rule beside it. */
nav[aria-label="Outline"] a[aria-current] { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; border-left-color: var(--accent); }
nav[aria-label="Outline"] li[data-level="2"] { padding-left: .9rem; }
nav[aria-label="Outline"] li[data-level="3"] { padding-left: 1.8rem; font-size: .95em; }
/* ⭐ An outline whose shallowest entries are level 3 (a unit with no level-2
   headings) starts them flush: the indent says "nested", and nothing is above them. */
nav[aria-label="Outline"] ol:not(:has(li[data-level="2"])) li[data-level="3"] { padding-left: 0; font-size: inherit; }

/* --- the two lists that ARE their pages ---------------------------------- */

nav[aria-label="Units"],
nav[aria-label="Contents"] {
  margin: 1rem 0 0;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: 1rem;
}

nav[aria-label="Units"] ol,
nav[aria-label="Contents"] ol {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

/* A row is navigation, not running text, so it takes the whole list's width
   and the per-row facts can sit right-aligned on it. */
nav[aria-label="Units"] li,
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li { max-width: none; }

/* A row: the tick box, then the ordinal in a fixed column, then the title.
   The feint line is drawn on each row, so the boxes (painted after it, on the
   page's own ground) sit ON it rather than behind it. */
nav[aria-label="Units"] li[id],
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li[id] {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .7rem;
  padding: .3rem 0;
  background: linear-gradient(var(--rule), var(--rule)) no-repeat calc(.45rem - .5px) 0 / 1px 100%;
}

nav[aria-label="Units"] li[id]::before,
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li[id]::before {
  content: "";
  flex: 0 0 .9rem;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  height: .9rem;
  border: 1.5px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
  background: var(--bg);
}

nav[aria-label="Units"] li > a,
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li > a {
  display: flex;
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  gap: .25rem;
  color: var(--fg);
  text-decoration: none;
}

nav[aria-label="Units"] li > a:hover,
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li > a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

nav[aria-label="Units"] span[data-kind="numbering"],
nav[aria-label="Contents"] span[data-kind="numbering"] {
  min-width: 1.9rem;
  color: var(--muted);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}

/* ⭐ Read: a solid ink box with a paper tick. ⚠️ `data-marked` is set at read
   time and emitted by no renderer — the mark is the reader's own, and the
   generated page is byte-identical whoever opens it (R10). */
nav[aria-label="Units"] li[data-marked="true"]::before,
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li[data-marked="true"]::before {
  content: "\2713";
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  font-size: .7rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  line-height: 1;
  color: var(--bg);
  background: var(--done);
  border-color: var(--done);
}

/* ⭐ Up next: the empty box outlined in the accent, and the words beside it.
   `aria-current="step"` is set by the page script on the first unread row. */
nav[aria-label="Units"] li[aria-current="step"]::before,
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li[aria-current="step"]::before {
  border-color: var(--accent);
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px var(--accent-soft);
}

nav[aria-label="Units"] li[aria-current="step"]::after,
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li[aria-current="step"]::after {
  content: "Up next";
  margin-left: auto;
  font-size: .85rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--accent);
}

/* ⛔ `data-readable="false"` is §7's third state — listed, not openable — and
   legible without colour: italic, no pointer affordance, a dashed box. */
nav[aria-label="Units"] li[data-readable="false"],
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li[data-readable="false"] {
  font-style: italic;
  color: var(--muted);
}

nav[aria-label="Units"] li[data-readable="false"]::before,
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li[data-readable="false"]::before { border-style: dashed; }

/* The index's groups: a summary per group with its own count, right-aligned
   (the page script writes the `<small>`), and the group's rows under it. */
nav[aria-label="Contents"] details { margin: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
nav[aria-label="Contents"] details details { border-top: 0; }

nav[aria-label="Contents"] summary {
  display: flex;
  align-items: baseline;
  gap: .5rem;
  padding: .75rem 0 .5rem;
  font-size: 1.12rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--fg);
  cursor: pointer;
}

nav[aria-label="Contents"] details details > summary { font-size: 1rem; padding: .5rem 0 .3rem; }

nav[aria-label="Contents"] span[data-kind="level"] {
  font-size: .85rem;
  font-weight: 400;
  color: var(--muted);
}

nav[aria-label="Contents"] summary > small {
  margin-left: auto;
  white-space: nowrap;
  font-size: .88rem;
  font-weight: 400;
  color: var(--muted);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}

nav[aria-label="Contents"] details > ol { padding-bottom: .6rem; }

/* --- the index's head: what this is, how to use it, where you are -------- */

/* ⭐ The reference's order of information, re-derived for a study site: the
   plain explanation (`notes.css`), then progress, then Up next, then the
   filter. */
/* The progress line: the count first and heavy, what is left right-aligned on
   the same line. Then the strip, one segment per group sized by its units,
   each a link to its group, filled to what is read, the one holding the next
   unit outlined in the accent. The page script fills both; with no script the
   whole region stays hidden, because the marks live in the browser. */
section[aria-label="Progress"] {
  margin: 1.75rem 0 0;
  padding: 1.1rem 1.25rem 1.25rem;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  box-shadow: var(--card-shadow);
}

section[aria-label="Progress"] > p {
  margin: 0 0 .6rem;
  font-size: 1.1rem;
  color: var(--muted);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  max-width: none;
}

section[aria-label="Progress"] strong { color: var(--fg); font-weight: 700; }
section[aria-label="Progress"] > p > span { float: right; font-size: .92rem; }

section[aria-label="Progress"] ol {
  display: flex;
  gap: 3px;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

/* ⛔ The floor keeps a one-unit group visible, and gives way to an equal share
   of the row when the segments outnumber the room for it: nothing overflows. */
section[aria-label="Progress"] li {
  flex: var(--units, 1) 1 0;
  min-width: min(.9rem, calc(100% / var(--segments, 1) - 3px));
}

section[aria-label="Progress"] li > a {
  display: block;
  font-size: .8rem;
  color: var(--muted);
  text-decoration: none;
  white-space: nowrap;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

/* The bar is the link's own first box, so the accent's ring goes round the
   bar and not round its label. */
section[aria-label="Progress"] li > a::before {
  content: "";
  display: block;
  height: .55rem;
  margin: 3px 3px .35rem;
  background: linear-gradient(to right, var(--done) var(--read, 0%), var(--surface-2) var(--read, 0%));
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
}

section[aria-label="Progress"] li > a:hover { color: var(--fg); }

section[aria-label="Progress"] li[aria-current="step"] > a { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; }

section[aria-label="Progress"] li[aria-current="step"] > a::before {
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--bg), 0 0 0 3px var(--accent);
}

/* The filter and the two separate controls (the brief's §3: expand and
   collapse are two buttons, each doing one thing). Hidden until the page
   script can make them work. */
form[role="search"] {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: .5rem;
  margin: 1.75rem 0 .5rem;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
}

form[role="search"] input {
  flex: 1 1 14rem;
  font: inherit;
  padding: .45rem .7rem;
  color: var(--fg);
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: 8px;
}

form[role="search"] input::placeholder { color: var(--muted); }

form[role="search"] button {
  font: inherit;
  font-size: .88rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  padding: .4rem .85rem;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: 8px;
  cursor: pointer;
}

form[role="search"] button:hover { background: var(--surface-2); }

form[role="search"] p {
  flex-basis: 100%;
  margin: 0;
  font-size: .9rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

/* ⭐ GROUP CARDS (the 2026-11 redesign). A course with a handful of top-level
   groups shows each as a card — its title, and its own bar filled to what is
   read — instead of one thin strip: the sample's "track" cards. ⛔ Only up to
   six groups: past that the strip above stays, because sixty cards are a wall
   and the strip is what `test_scale` holds to one row. */
section[aria-label="Progress"] ol:not(:has(> li:nth-child(7))) {
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: .75rem;
  margin-top: 1rem;
}
/* ⚠️ A wrapping flex row, not a grid: the page's layout is the one grid
   `chrome.css` declares under its threshold, and a card row is not a layout. */
section[aria-label="Progress"] ol:not(:has(> li:nth-child(7))) > li { flex: 1 1 12rem; min-width: 0; }
section[aria-label="Progress"] ol:not(:has(> li:nth-child(7))) > li > a {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  height: 100%;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  padding: .85rem 1rem .9rem;
  font-size: .95rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  line-height: 1.3;
  color: var(--fg);
  white-space: normal;
  background: var(--bg);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: 10px;
}
section[aria-label="Progress"] ol:not(:has(> li:nth-child(7))) > li > a::before {
  order: 2;
  height: 6px;
  margin: auto 0 0;
  padding-top: 0;
  border-top: .75rem solid var(--bg);
  box-sizing: content-box;
  border-radius: 3px;
}
section[aria-label="Progress"] ol:not(:has(> li:nth-child(7))) > li > a:hover { border-color: var(--rule-strong); }
section[aria-label="Progress"] ol:not(:has(> li:nth-child(7))) > li[aria-current="step"] > a { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--fg); }
section[aria-label="Progress"] ol:not(:has(> li:nth-child(7))) > li[aria-current="step"] > a::before { box-shadow: none; }

/* ⭐ The index's top-level groups each stand on a card, their rows inside. */
nav[aria-label="Contents"] > ol > li > details {
  margin: 0 0 1rem;
  padding: 0 1.1rem .4rem;
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  box-shadow: var(--card-shadow);
}
nav[aria-label="Contents"] > ol > li > details > summary { padding: 1rem 0 .7rem; }
nav[aria-label="Contents"] > ol > li > details[open] > summary { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); margin-bottom: .3rem; }
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li[id] { padding: .5rem 0; background: none; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li[id]:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li[id]::before { border-radius: var(--radius-control); }
nav[aria-label="Contents"] li > a { align-items: baseline; }
nav[aria-label="Contents"] span[data-kind="numbering"] { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-variant-ligatures: none; font-feature-settings: "liga" 0, "calt" 0; font-size: .8rem; }

/* Where the reader goes next: the unit's footer card and the Up next slip.

   ⭐ ONE OF THE CHROME PARTS (`chrome.css` says how they divide). It is
   its own part because it holds THE ONE BOLD ELEMENT: the next link, filled
   with `--sign` on exactly one element per page and nowhere else in any part
   (`test_identity` asserts it). Everything else on the page uses the accent as
   a line, a ring or a word.

   ⭐ THE 2026-11 REDESIGN: ONE FOOTER CARD. A unit page ends on a card that
   holds the read control (`read-mark.html`), the way up to the unit's list and
   Previous / next. The next unit is the card's primary button — still the
   sign-filled slip — and, while the unit is unmarked, `read-mark.js` labels it
   *Mark read & continue* and marks the unit as the reader follows it. ⛔ With
   no script the read control stays hidden, the bar is the whole card, and the
   next link is a plain link that navigates.

   ⚠️ The read control and the bar are two regions of the skeleton (the
   narration transport may stand between them), so the card is drawn as two
   halves: the control's top half and the bar's bottom half, joined only when
   the control is shown.

   ⛔ No colour, no class, no second width threshold: see `chrome.css`. The two
   columns of Previous and next stack by wrapping, never by a media query. */

/* --- the read control: the top half of the card ---------------------------- */

/* ⭐ The reader's own record: a button that looks like one, with the
   tick box it sets drawn inside it — empty, then solid ink once pressed — and
   one quiet line about where the mark lives. ⚠️ The region ships `hidden` and
   `read-mark.js` unhides it only with a working store. The state is told apart
   by the box and the words, not by colour alone. */
body > section[data-section="read-mark"] {
  margin: 2.5rem 0 0;
  padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem .25rem;
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-bottom: 0;
  border-radius: var(--radius-card) var(--radius-card) 0 0;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
}

body > section[data-section="read-mark"] button {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .55rem;
  font: inherit;
  font-weight: 700;
  padding: .5rem 1rem;
  color: var(--fg);
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
  cursor: pointer;
}

body > section[data-section="read-mark"] button:hover { background: var(--surface-2); }

body > section[data-section="read-mark"] button::before {
  content: "";
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  width: .95rem;
  height: .95rem;
  font-size: .7rem;
  line-height: 1;
  border: 1.5px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
}

body > section[data-section="read-mark"][data-marked="true"] button::before {
  content: "\2713";
  color: var(--bg);
  background: var(--done);
  border-color: var(--done);
}

body > section[data-section="read-mark"] p {
  margin: .6rem 0 0;
  font-size: .85rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

body > section[data-section="read-mark"] p:first-child { margin: 0; }

/* --- the between-pages bar: the bottom half, or the whole card ------------- */

/* ⭐ The way up is a quiet line of its own across the top; Previous and next
   are two cards side by side, each a small label over its title. ⛔ No arrow
   glyphs: each carries an inline SVG arrow (`link-*.html`). Told apart by
   face, size, weight, layout and the slip's fill — never a colour alone. */
nav[aria-label="Between units"] {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: .75rem;
  margin: 2.5rem 0;
  padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem 1.5rem;
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  box-shadow: var(--card-shadow);
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: 1rem;
  font-weight: 700;
}

/* Joined to the read control above it: no gap, no top edge, and a hairline
   inside the card between the two halves. */
section[data-section="read-mark"]:not([hidden]) ~ nav[aria-label="Between units"] {
  margin-top: 0;
  border-top: 0;
  border-radius: 0 0 var(--radius-card) var(--radius-card);
}

section[data-section="read-mark"]:not([hidden]) ~ nav[aria-label="Between units"]::before {
  content: "";
  flex: 1 0 100%;
  order: 0;
  height: 1px;
  margin: 0 0 .5rem;
  background: var(--rule);
}

nav[aria-label="Between units"] a {
  min-width: 0;
  color: var(--fg);
  text-decoration: none;
}

nav[aria-label="Between units"] a > span,
nav[aria-label="Up next"] a > span {
  display: block;
  font-size: .8rem;
  font-weight: 400;
  color: var(--muted);
}

/* The way up: one quiet line, the label and the list's name on it. */
nav[aria-label="Between units"] a[rel="up"] {
  flex: 1 0 100%;
  order: 1;
  overflow: hidden;
  font-size: .9rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-decoration-color: var(--rule-strong);
  text-underline-offset: .2em;
}

nav[aria-label="Between units"] a[rel="up"] > span {
  display: inline;
  margin-right: .35rem;
  font-size: inherit;
}

nav[aria-label="Between units"] a[rel="up"]:hover { color: var(--fg); }

/* Previous and next: two cards, side by side while there is room, each with
   its arrow in its padding. */
nav[aria-label="Between units"] a:is([rel="prev"], [data-pager="previous"], [rel="next"], [data-pager="next"]) {
  position: relative;
  display: block;
  flex: 1 1 15rem;
  order: 2;
  padding: .75rem 1rem .8rem 2.85rem;
  line-height: 1.3;
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
}

nav[aria-label="Between units"] a:is([rel="prev"], [data-pager="previous"]):hover {
  border-color: var(--rule-strong);
  background: var(--surface-2);
}

nav[aria-label="Between units"] a > svg {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 1rem;
  margin-top: -9px;
  color: var(--muted);
}

/* THE SLIP: the next unit, the card's primary button, its arrow on the right. */
nav[aria-label="Between units"] a:is([rel="next"], [data-pager="next"]) { padding: .75rem 2.85rem .8rem 1rem; }

nav[aria-label="Between units"] a:is([rel="next"], [data-pager="next"]) > svg { left: auto; right: 1rem; }

nav[aria-label="Between units"] a[rel="next"] {
  order: 3;
  color: var(--sign-ink);
  background: var(--sign);
  border-color: var(--sign);
}

nav[aria-label="Between units"] a[rel="next"]:hover { color: var(--sign-ink); text-decoration: underline; }

nav[aria-label="Between units"] a[rel="next"] > :is(span, svg) {
  color: var(--sign-ink);
  font-weight: 600;
}

/* The label the script is not showing (*Up next* or *Mark read & continue*). */
nav[aria-label="Between units"] a > span[hidden] { display: none; }

/* ⚠️ A chain's other neighbours ship `hidden`; this comes after the card rules
   above, which would otherwise outweigh the attribute. */
nav[aria-label="Between units"] a:is([rel], [data-pager])[hidden] { display: none; }

/* --- the Up next slip of a container page and the root index --------------- */

/* ⭐ The next page in the order, as THE SLIP: the one filled area of the sign
   colour on the page, with its label on a tab on its top edge. */
nav[aria-label="Up next"] a,
nav[aria-label="Up next"] > p {
  position: relative;
  display: block;
  margin-top: 1.6rem;
  padding: 1rem 1.25rem 1.1rem;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: 1.45rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  line-height: 1.2;
  color: var(--sign-ink);
  background: var(--sign);
  border-radius: var(--radius-sign) 0 var(--radius-sign) var(--radius-sign);
  text-decoration: none;
  text-wrap: balance;
}

nav[aria-label="Up next"] a:hover { text-decoration: underline; color: var(--sign-ink); }

/* The tab: the slip's label, standing on its top edge at the right. */
nav[aria-label="Up next"] a > span,
nav[aria-label="Up next"] > p > span {
  position: absolute;
  right: 0;
  bottom: 100%;
  padding: .2rem .8rem .15rem;
  font-size: .85rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--sign-ink);
  background: var(--sign);
  border-radius: var(--radius-sign) var(--radius-sign) 0 0;
}

nav[aria-label="Up next"] { margin: 1.25rem 0 0; }

/* ⛔ A container page carries its own Up next (the first unread unit in it),
   so the bar's next CONTAINER steps down to a quiet card there: one slip per
   page, never two. */
body:has(nav[aria-label="Up next"]) nav[aria-label="Between units"] a[rel="next"] {
  color: var(--fg);
  background: none;
  border-color: var(--rule);
}

body:has(nav[aria-label="Up next"]) nav[aria-label="Between units"] a[rel="next"] > :is(span, svg) {
  color: var(--muted);
  font-weight: 400;
}

/* What the page tells the reader, and the control it offers: the index's
   plain explanation, the practices note and the read control.

   ⭐ ONE OF THE FOUR CHROME PARTS (`chrome.css` says how they divide).

   ⛔ NOTES, NOT PANELS. Both regions were tinted, rounded panels — one with a
   coloured stripe down its left edge, the brief's tell (P1, RM1). They are now
   lines in the column: a feint rule above, the interface face, and nothing
   else. ⛔ `body >` scopes every selector, because `data-section` is
   OVERLOADED: `section.html` carries a corpus's own key in it (R1). No colour
   and no class, as in the other two parts. */

/* --- practices still to come ------------------------------------------- */

/* ⛔ Without this the page lies by omission: a unit whose practices were never
   ingested renders exactly like a unit that has none. So it must not read as
   more prose — it is told apart by its face, its size, its heading and the rule
   above it, and it is written in pencil (`--practice`), not a second accent. */
body > section[data-section="practices-pending"] {
  margin: 2.5rem 0 0;
  padding: .75rem 1rem .85rem;
  background: var(--practice-soft);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  color: var(--practice);
}

body > section[data-section="practices-pending"] h2 {
  margin: 0 0 .2rem;
  font-size: 1rem;
  color: var(--practice);
}

body > section[data-section="practices-pending"] p {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: .95rem;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
}

/* --- the read control ---------------------------------------------------- */

/* ⭐ Moved to `onward.css` in the 2026-11 redesign: the read control is the top
   of the unit's footer card, with the between-units bar under it. */

/* --- the index's plain explanation ------------------------------------- */

/* ⭐ One plain paragraph, then two short columns — how to use the site and how
   it is ordered — which share the width when there is room and stack when
   there is not, by flex-wrap, so no second width threshold is declared.
   ⚠️ No "where it comes from" column: that is the corpus's own fact and waits
   for manifest data that declares it. */
section[aria-label="About this site"] {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 0 2.5rem;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: .95rem;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
}

section[aria-label="About this site"] > p {
  flex: 1 1 100%;
  margin: 0 0 1rem;
  font-family: var(--font-prose);
  font-size: 1.2rem;
}

section[aria-label="About this site"] > section {
  flex: 1 1 16rem;
  padding-top: .8rem;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
}
section[aria-label="About this site"] h2 { margin: .2rem 0 .4rem; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--fg); }
section[aria-label="About this site"] ol { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; }
section[aria-label="About this site"] li,
section[aria-label="About this site"] p { margin: 0 0 .35rem; max-width: none; }

/* The theme control is one icon button in the top bar, styled in `topbar.css`. */

/* The top bar: always on screen, carrying the search, the reading-mode switch and the theme.

   ⛔ NOT ONE COLOUR AND NOT ONE CLASS. Every value is a `palette.css` token and every
   part is reached by its role, its label or a published `data-*` hook.

   ⭐ INSIDE THE MASTHEAD, FIXED TO THE WINDOW. `page.html` declares the top-level positions
   of `<body>` that the rail's grid row span counts, so the bar is a child of `<header>` and
   leaves the flow with `position: fixed`: it adds no row and no grid cell. `body` takes the
   bar's height as top padding so nothing starts underneath it, `html` takes it as scroll
   padding so a heading opened by its address is not hidden behind it, and the rail and the
   outline stick just below it.

   ⛔ Its layer is 2: above the page and the skip link, below the practice workspace (3 and 4),
   which is a full-window mode with a bar of its own. The search dialog (`search.css`) is above
   everything. */

html { scroll-padding-top: calc(var(--topbar) + .75rem); }

body { padding-top: var(--topbar); }

body > header > div[role="toolbar"][data-section="topbar"] {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  z-index: 2;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .75rem;
  height: var(--topbar);
  padding: 0 var(--gutter);
  background: var(--surface);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
}

/* The search sits at the left edge of the bar; the mode switch and the theme icon at the right. */
/* ⭐ THE THREE ZONES (the 2026-11 redesign): the course's name at the left,
   its top-level sections in the middle, search and the theme at the right. The
   markup keeps its order (brand, sections, search, theme); the zones are the
   flex row's, so a page without a brand or sections still lays out. */
body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > a[data-section="brand"] {
  flex: 0 1 auto;
  min-width: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
  color: var(--fg);
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 1rem;
  letter-spacing: -.01em;
  text-decoration: none;
  max-width: 18rem;
  margin-right: 1rem;
}
body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > nav[data-section="sections"] { flex: 1 1 auto; display: flex; justify-content: safe center; min-width: 0; overflow: hidden; }
body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > nav[data-section="sections"] ul { display: flex; gap: 1.5rem; margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; min-width: 0; }
/* ⚠️ A corpus may title a section with a whole sentence: each link gives way
   with an ellipsis rather than pushing the bar past the window. */
body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > nav[data-section="sections"] li { min-width: 0; flex: 0 1 auto; }
body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > nav[data-section="sections"] a {
  color: var(--fg-soft);
  font-size: .9rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  text-decoration: none;
  white-space: nowrap;
  display: block;
  max-width: 14rem;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  padding: .3rem 0;
  border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
}
body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > nav[data-section="sections"] a:hover { color: var(--fg); }
body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > nav[data-section="sections"] a[aria-current] { color: var(--accent); border-bottom-color: var(--accent); }
/* ⛔ The sections fold away where they would crowd the brand; the rail and the
   index still list them. */
@media (max-width: 60rem) {
  body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > nav[data-section="sections"] { display: none; }
}
body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > a[data-section="brand"] + button[data-section="search-open"],
body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > nav[data-section="sections"] + button[data-section="search-open"] { margin-left: auto; }
body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > button[data-section="search-open"] {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .5rem;
  width: min(16rem, 40vw);
  padding: .35rem .6rem;
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: var(--bg);
  color: var(--muted);
  font: inherit;
  font-size: .88rem;
  cursor: pointer;
}

body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > button[data-section="search-open"][hidden] { display: none; }

body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > button[data-section="search-open"]:hover {
  color: var(--fg);
  background: var(--surface-2);
}

body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > button[data-section="search-open"] span { flex: 1 1 auto; text-align: left; }

body > header > div[role="toolbar"] kbd {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  padding: 0 .35rem;
  border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
  background: var(--surface);
  color: var(--fg-soft);
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: .72rem;
  line-height: 1.5;
}

/* --- the theme icon ------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ⭐ One button that shows the theme the page is in: the sun on a light page, the moon on a dark
   one, and pressing it chooses the other. ⛔ Chosen by CSS alone from the same `data-theme`
   attribute and system scheme `palette.css` answers to, so the icon is right on the first paint. */
body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > button[data-section="theme"] {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  width: 2.25rem;
  height: 2.25rem;
  padding: 0;
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: var(--bg);
  color: var(--fg);
  cursor: pointer;
}

body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > button[data-section="theme"][hidden] { display: none; }

body > header > div[role="toolbar"] > button[data-section="theme"]:hover { background: var(--surface-2); }

button[data-section="theme"] svg[data-icon="moon"] { display: none; }

:root[data-theme="dark"] button[data-section="theme"] svg[data-icon="sun"] { display: none; }
:root[data-theme="dark"] button[data-section="theme"] svg[data-icon="moon"] { display: block; }

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root:not([data-theme="light"]) button[data-section="theme"] svg[data-icon="sun"] { display: none; }
  :root:not([data-theme="light"]) button[data-section="theme"] svg[data-icon="moon"] { display: block; }
}

/* The page outside the reading mode: a note under the bar in the masthead, in the flow. */
body > header aside[data-section="mode-outside"] { max-width: var(--measure); }

/* --- the rail and the outline start under the bar ------------------------- */

@media (min-width: 72rem) {
  body > nav[aria-label="Containers"],
  body:has(nav[aria-label="Containers"]) > nav[aria-label="Outline"],
  body:has(nav[aria-label="Containers"]) > section[aria-label="About this site"] {
    top: var(--topbar);
    max-height: calc(100vh - var(--topbar));
  }
}

/* ⭐ THE READING LINE: a 2px accent line under the bar, as long as the share of
   the page already read (`reading-progress.js` sets its width). With scripting
   off it stays at nothing. */
body > header > div[data-section="reading-progress"] {
  position: fixed;
  top: var(--topbar);
  left: 0;
  z-index: 2;
  height: 2px;
  width: 0;
  background: var(--accent);
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* The search dialog: a box over the page, the results grouped by page beneath it.

   ⛔ NOT ONE COLOUR AND NOT ONE CLASS. Palette tokens only, so both themes read it; every part
   is reached by its `data-*` hook, and the matched words are `<mark>` elements the part writes.

   ⭐ ABOVE EVERYTHING (layer 40): the top bar is 2 and the practice workspace 3 and 4. The
   dialog sits in the masthead and leaves the flow with `position: fixed`, so it adds no row to
   the page grid. ⛔ The results list is the one thing in it that scrolls, and only when its
   rows do not fit; the page behind it is left to scroll as it always does. */

body > header > div[role="dialog"][data-section="search"] {
  position: fixed;
  z-index: 40;
  inset: 0;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  padding: 10vh var(--gutter) 0;
  background: var(--scrim);
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
}

body > header > div[role="dialog"][data-section="search"][hidden] { display: none; }

body > header > div[role="dialog"][data-section="search"] > * {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  width: min(42rem, 100%);
  margin: 0;
  background: var(--panel);
  color: var(--fg);
  border-left: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-right: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
}

[data-search-box] {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .6rem;
  padding: .7rem .9rem;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-sign) var(--radius-sign) 0 0;
  color: var(--muted);
}

[data-search-box] input {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-width: 0;
  padding: .3rem 0;
  border: 0;
  background: none;
  color: var(--fg);
  font: inherit;
  font-size: 1.05rem;
}

[data-search-box] input:focus { outline: none; }

[data-search-box]:focus-within { box-shadow: inset 0 -2px 0 var(--accent); }

button[data-search-close] {
  padding: .1rem .45rem;
  border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
  background: var(--surface);
  color: var(--fg-soft);
  font: inherit;
  font-size: .75rem;
  cursor: pointer;
}

[data-search-results] {
  max-height: min(60vh, 34rem);
  overflow-y: auto;
  padding: 0 .5rem;
}

[data-search-results]:empty { display: none; }

[data-search-results] [role="group"] { padding: .55rem 0 .25rem; }

[data-search-results] [role="group"] + [role="group"] { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }

[data-search-results] [data-search-page] {
  margin: 0 .5rem .25rem;
  font-size: .95rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--fg);
}

[data-search-results] [data-search-page] small {
  display: block;
  font-weight: 400;
  font-size: .78rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

[data-search-results] a[role="option"] {
  display: block;
  margin: 0 0 2px;
  padding: .4rem .5rem;
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
  color: var(--fg-soft);
  text-decoration: none;
  font-size: .86rem;
  line-height: 1.45;
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}

[data-search-results] a[role="option"] strong {
  display: block;
  color: var(--fg);
  font-size: .9rem;
}

[data-search-results] a[role="option"]:hover { background: var(--surface-2); }

/* ⛔ The chosen row is told apart by the wash AND a bar at its edge, never by colour alone. */
[data-search-results] a[role="option"][aria-selected="true"] {
  background: var(--accent-soft);
  box-shadow: inset 3px 0 0 var(--accent);
  color: var(--fg);
}

[data-search-results] mark {
  padding: 0 .1rem;
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
  background: var(--hl-bg);
  color: var(--hl-fg);
  font-weight: 700;
  box-shadow: inset 0 -2px 0 var(--hl-bar);
}

[data-search-status] {
  padding: .5rem .9rem;
  font-size: .85rem;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
}

[data-search-status]:empty { display: none; }

[data-search-hints] {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: .25rem 1rem;
  padding: .45rem .9rem;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: 0 0 var(--radius-sign) var(--radius-sign);
  font-size: .75rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

body > header p[data-search-hints],
body > header p[data-search-status] { max-width: none; font-size: .8rem; }

/* The practice panel: where the reader's work sits, and what happened when they
   ran it.

   ⛔ NO CLASS, and that is the standing rule for a region the framework itself
   emits: the panel is reached by `data-practice`, which `render/page/practice.py`
   is the single emitter of. ⚠️ Deliberately NOT `data-section` — `section.html`
   carries a CORPUS's own key in that attribute (R1), so a corpus that called a
   section `practice` would otherwise be styled as the framework's panel.

   ⛔ NO COLOUR AND NO MEASURE OF ITS OWN. Every value below is a palette token;
   the ground is `--practice-soft` and the ink `--practice`, which is the same
   pencil the practices note is written in — the panel is the reader's workbench
   and not a second accent competing with the prose.

   ⭐ A NOTE, THEN CONTROLS, THEN A TERMINAL. The three parts read top to bottom
   in the order a reader uses them, and every one of them is a plain element: the
   rule above the panel tells it apart from the section it belongs to without a
   box, exactly as the two notes in `notes.css` are told apart. */

section[data-practice] {
  margin: 2rem 0 0;
  padding: .85rem 1rem 1rem;
  background: var(--practice-soft);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  color: var(--practice);
}

section[data-practice] h2 {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 1rem;
  color: var(--practice);
}

/* ⛔ The measure is NOT reopened here. `reading.css` caps `main p` at
   `--measure` and the panel sits inside the reading column, so its sentences
   break where every other sentence on the page does. ⚠️ Lifting that cap lays
   the panel's own explanation out as one line of hundreds of characters, which
   `tests/visual/test_reading_room.py` reads and no stylesheet test could. */
section[data-practice] p {
  margin: 0 0 .5rem;
  font-size: .95rem;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
}

section[data-practice] code {
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  font-variant-ligatures: none;
  font-feature-settings: "liga" 0, "calt" 0;
  font-size: .9em;
  color: var(--fg);
}

/* ⛔ The editor slot says the editor is not running and how to start it, rather
   than rendering blank: an IDE with a shell is not started because somebody
   opened a reading page, and a blank frame looks broken. */
section[data-practice] div[data-practice-part="editor"] {
  margin: 0 0 .5rem;
  padding: .7rem .85rem;
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px dashed var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
}

/* ⚠️ `> p`, not `p:last-child`: the tablist and the two frames now follow the
   sentence inside this slot, so the sentence stopped being the last child the
   day they were added — and a rule keyed on position would have gone quietly
   dead rather than red. It is the only paragraph directly in here, and it is
   hidden whenever a frame is showing. */
section[data-practice] div[data-practice-part="editor"] > p { margin: 0; }

/* ⭐ The two windows of ONE editor — *Your code* and *Tests* — one visible at a
   time, never a split pane: the file a reader may type in and the file that
   judges it are two different acts of reading, and standing them side by side
   halves the width of both. ⚠️ Real buttons in a real tablist, so they are in
   the tab order and announced as tabs. */
section[data-practice] div[data-practice-part="tabs"] {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: .4rem;
  margin: 0 0 .5rem;
}

/* ⛔ The selected tab is told apart by INK AND WEIGHT as well as by its ground,
   so it is not colour alone that says which window a reader is looking at. */
section[data-practice] button[role="tab"][aria-selected="true"] {
  color: var(--practice);
  background: var(--practice-soft);
  border-color: var(--practice);
}

/* ⭐ The frames `practice-editor.js` puts a RUNNING editor's two windows in,
   and they exist only on a served page: a built one names no origin
   (R8). ⚠️ A box of its own, for the reason the output has one — an editor
   given no height is a 150-pixel-tall development environment. ⛔ No colour of
   its own: the ground is the same surface token the slot already stands on. */
section[data-practice] div[data-practice-part="editor"] iframe {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: 30rem;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
  background: var(--surface-2);
}

/* The two acts and the stop. ⚠️ Real buttons, so they are in the tab order, take
   Enter and Space, and are announced as buttons — none of which a styled `div`
   would be. */
section[data-practice] p[data-practice-part="controls"] {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: .5rem;
  align-items: center;
}

section[data-practice] button {
  font: inherit;
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: .95rem;
  padding: .4rem .95rem;
  color: var(--fg);
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
  cursor: pointer;
}

section[data-practice] button:hover:not([disabled]) { background: var(--surface-2); }

/* ⚠️ A run in flight, not a control that can never work: the panel emits no
   button it cannot honour, and this state is told apart by the cursor and the
   ink as well as by the opacity. */
section[data-practice] button[disabled] {
  color: var(--muted);
  cursor: default;
  opacity: .65;
}

section[data-practice] p[data-practice-part="status"] {
  margin: .5rem 0 0;
  font-size: .9rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

/* ⛔ The program's own output, and it may be wide: it scrolls inside its own box
   or the page scrolls sideways and every paragraph with it — the argument the
   table wrapper already makes. ⭐ `tabindex="0"` is in the markup so a keyboard
   reader can scroll it without a mouse. */
section[data-practice] pre[data-practice-part="output"] {
  margin: .6rem 0 0;
  max-height: 20rem;
  overflow: auto;
  padding: .6rem .75rem;
  background: var(--code-bg);
  color: var(--code-fg);
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  font-variant-ligatures: none;
  font-feature-settings: "liga" 0, "calt" 0;
  font-size: .85rem;
  line-height: 1.5;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
}

/* ⭐ **WHAT THE SUBMIT REPORTED**: every case this practice declares,
   marked with what the run said about it, under one sentence that counts the
   edges. ⛔ It sits between the status line and the raw output on purpose — the
   reader's question after a Submit is *what is still missing*, and the grader's
   own log is the place they go next rather than first.

   ⛔ **TOLD APART BY A WORD, NEVER BY COLOUR ALONE.** Each row carries *Passed*
   or *Not yet* as text, put there by `practice.js` out of the markup's own
   attributes — so the breakdown reads the same to a screen reader, in a
   high-contrast theme and on a printed page. ⚠️ The ink below is a second
   signal and never the only one.

   ⛔ **NO NEW TOKEN AND NO NEW COLOUR.** `--done` is the ink a finished thing is
   already marked in on this site and `--accent` is the one that asks for
   attention; a green and a red invented here would be two more values nobody
   checked against either theme. */
section[data-practice] div[data-practice-part="breakdown"] {
  margin: .6rem 0 0;
  padding: .6rem .85rem;
  background: var(--surface);
  border-left: 3px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
}

section[data-practice] div[data-practice-part="breakdown"] p[data-practice-part="summary"] {
  margin: 0 0 .4rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--practice);
}

section[data-practice] ul[data-practice-part="cases"] {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
  font-size: .9rem;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
}

section[data-practice] li[data-practice-case] { margin: 0 0 .25rem; }

/* ⭐ The word, then the sentence the CORPUS wrote (R1). ⚠️ `inline-block` with a
   measured width so the sentences line up as a column rather than as ragged
   prose — the one thing that makes a list of verdicts readable at a glance. */
section[data-practice] li[data-practice-case] span[data-practice-part="verdict"] {
  display: inline-block;
  min-width: 5rem;
  font-weight: 700;
}

/* ⭐ The 2026-11 redesign: a case's verdict is a status, so it takes the status inks — always
   beside its word ("Done", "Not yet", "Skipped"), never colour alone. A skipped case (it
   declared `skips_without` and the run lacked that) is neither: quiet and slanted. */
section[data-practice] li[data-practice-case][data-practice-verdict="passed"] span {
  color: var(--ok);
}

section[data-practice] li[data-practice-case][data-practice-verdict="failed"] span {
  color: var(--bad);
}

section[data-practice] li[data-practice-case][data-practice-verdict="skipped"] span {
  color: var(--muted);
  font-style: italic;
  font-weight: 400;
}

/* ⭐ The link that opens the editor in a tab of its own is a line of its own above the frame, so
   it is seen whether or not the tablist above it is shown. */
section[data-practice] a[data-practice-popout] {
  display: block;
  margin: 0 0 .5rem;
  font-size: .9rem;
}

/* ⭐ **A project's explorer is the tablist, laid out as a folder tree** (`render/page/project.py`):
   one button per file, a folder's name above its files, read-only files told apart by a word as
   well as by ink. Beside the frame when the workspace is open, above it otherwise. */
section[data-practice] div[data-practice-explorer] {
  flex-direction: column;
  flex-wrap: nowrap;
  align-items: stretch;
  gap: .15rem;
  max-height: 24rem;
  overflow: auto;
}

section[data-practice] div[data-practice-explorer] p[data-practice-folder] {
  margin: .4rem 0 0;
  font-size: .8rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

section[data-practice] div[data-practice-explorer] button {
  text-align: left;
}

section[data-practice] div[data-practice-explorer] button[data-practice-readonly]::after,
section[data-practice] [data-practice-part="tree"] span[data-practice-readonly] {
  font-size: .75rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

section[data-practice] div[data-practice-explorer] button[data-practice-readonly]::after {
  content: " read-only";
}

html[data-workspace-open] section[data-practice][data-workspace-open]
  div[data-practice-part="editor"]:has(> div[data-practice-explorer]:not([hidden])) {
  grid-template-columns: minmax(9rem, 15rem) minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
}

html[data-workspace-open] section[data-practice][data-workspace-open]
  div[data-practice-part="editor"] > div[data-practice-explorer] {
  grid-column: 1;
  grid-row: 2;
  align-self: stretch;
  max-height: none;
}

html[data-workspace-open] section[data-practice][data-workspace-open]
  div[data-practice-part="editor"]:has(> div[data-practice-explorer]:not([hidden]))
  div[data-practice-frame] {
  grid-column: 2 / -1;
}

/* What a run reported about each case: its failure message and the text the reader's code wrote (`practice-detail.js`).
   ⛔ No new token and no new colour: the message and the text sit in the same box the program's own output does,
   and a folded section is a `details` element, so a keyboard and a screen reader open it as they open any other. */
section[data-practice] [data-practice-part="case-detail"] {
  margin: .3rem 0 .5rem 5rem;
}

section[data-practice] [data-practice-part="case-message"],
section[data-practice] [data-practice-part$="-lines"] {
  margin: .25rem 0 0;
  max-height: 14rem;
  overflow: auto;
  padding: .45rem .65rem;
  background: var(--code-bg);
  color: var(--code-fg);
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  font-variant-ligatures: none;
  font-feature-settings: "liga" 0, "calt" 0;
  font-size: .8rem;
  line-height: 1.45;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
}

section[data-practice] [data-practice-part="case-detail"] details,
section[data-practice] [data-practice-part="run-detail"] details {
  margin: .25rem 0 0;
}

section[data-practice] [data-practice-part="case-detail"] summary,
section[data-practice] [data-practice-part="run-detail"] summary {
  cursor: pointer;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
}

section[data-practice] [data-practice-part="run-detail"] { margin: .5rem 0 0; }

section[data-practice] [data-practice-part="run-note"] {
  margin: .3rem 0 0;
  font-size: .85rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

/* The quiz: the questions, the answers a reader may choose, and what each one says.

   ⛔ NO CLASS, and that is the standing rule for a region the framework itself
   emits: the section is reached by `data-practice-quiz`, which
   `render/page/quiz.py` is the single emitter of. ⚠️ Deliberately NOT
   `data-practice` — the code panel wears that, and the two shapes share a look
   but not a surface: a quiz has no editor slot, no controls row and no terminal,
   so a selector that covered both would carry rules for parts that are not
   there. ⭐ The one exception is the head's `.eyebrow`, the kind label every
   card of the 2026-11 redesign carries, and the only class allowed capitals.

   ⛔ **ITS OWN PART, and the split is the same one the script took**:
   `practice.css` is *the panel, the editor and the run*; this is *the
   questions*. ⚠️ It follows `practice.css` in `pageassets.bundle` so the two
   read together, and it shares every one of that file's rules about colour —
   ⛔ **no colour and no measure of its own**, every value a palette token.

   ⭐ **THE 2026-11 REDESIGN: A CARD OF OPTION CARDS.** The quiz stands on a
   card (`--radius-card`, a hairline, `--card-shadow`), each question on a
   quieter panel inside it, and each option is a full-width card whose WHOLE
   ROW is the label. After Check, the chosen option turns `--ok` or `--bad` —
   ⛔ never colour alone: it carries an icon and the verdict's word, and the
   sentence under the question starts with the same word.

   ⭐ The exam form (`mock-form.css`) wears these rules too: a quiz drawn one
   question at a time is the same card and the same options.

   ⛔ **NO DEAD CONTROL ANYWHERE BELOW.** There is no rule here for a disabled
   Run or a greyed Submit, because the page emits neither: a quiz has no file to
   open, no command to run and no grader to submit to. */

section[data-practice-quiz] {
  margin: 2rem 0 0;
  padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem 1.4rem;
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  box-shadow: var(--card-shadow);
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  color: var(--fg);
}

/* The kind label: *Concept check*, a drawn dot, and how many questions. ⛔ The
   dot is a 3px circle, never a middle-dot character. */
section[data-practice-quiz] .eyebrow {
  margin: 0 0 .3rem;
  font-size: .75rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: .08em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--accent);
}

section[data-practice-quiz] p.eyebrow > span + span::before {
  content: "";
  display: inline-block;
  width: 3px;
  height: 3px;
  margin: 0 .5rem;
  vertical-align: middle;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: currentColor;
}

section[data-practice-quiz] h2 {
  margin: 0 0 .35rem;
  font-size: 1.3rem;
  line-height: 1.3;
  color: var(--fg);
}

section[data-practice-quiz] p {
  margin: 0 0 .5rem;
  font-size: .95rem;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
}

/* "2 of 4 answered": the line the script keeps up to date as options are
   chosen, and the rule under the head. */
section[data-practice-quiz] p[data-practice-part="count"] {
  margin: .5rem 0 1rem;
  padding: 0 0 .9rem;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  font-size: .85rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

/* ⭐ The questions are an ordered list and they keep their numbers: a reader
   answering seven of them needs to know which one the sentence under it belongs
   to.

   ⛔ **The number is drawn IN THE LEGEND, never as the list's own marker**
   (the first corpus's finding F9). A marker sits on the list item's
   FIRST LINE BOX, and a `<legend>` is laid out in its fieldset's border rather
   than as a line of content — so the browser's marker landed beside the FIRST
   OPTION and the stem stood unnumbered above it. ⭐ A counter in the legend's
   `::before` puts the number on the stem's own line, whatever the browser does
   with a legend. ⚠️ It is still an `<ol>`, so the list and its length are still
   announced, and the legend's text — the group's name — now starts with the
   number. */
section[data-practice-quiz] ol[data-practice-part="questions"] {
  margin: 0 0 1rem;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
  counter-reset: question;
}

/* Each question on its own quieter panel inside the card. */
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-question] {
  margin: 0 0 1rem;
  padding: 1rem 1rem 1.05rem;
  background: var(--bg);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  counter-increment: question;
}

section[data-practice-quiz] legend::before {
  content: counter(question) ". ";
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  font-variant-ligatures: none;
  font-feature-settings: "liga" 0, "calt" 0;
  font-size: .85em;
  color: var(--accent);
}

/* ⛔ A REAL `<fieldset>` AND A REAL `<legend>`, so the stem is announced as the
   group's question before each option is read — which a styled `div` around
   loose radios is not. ⚠️ The border is removed and the grouping is not: the
   element is what carries the meaning, and the look is the list's. */
section[data-practice-quiz] fieldset {
  min-width: 0;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  border: 0;
}

section[data-practice-quiz] legend {
  padding: 0;
  font-weight: 700;
  line-height: 1.5;
  color: var(--fg);
}

section[data-practice-quiz] ul[data-practice-part="options"] {
  display: grid;
  gap: .5rem;
  margin: .75rem 0 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-option] { margin: 0; }

/* ⭐ The whole option is the label, so the hit area is the whole card and not
   the twelve pixels of the radio — which is the difference between a control
   that works on a phone and one that does not. Hover, chosen and focus are
   three different looks: a darker hairline, an accent edge on a soft ground,
   and the focus ring around the card. */
section[data-practice-quiz] label {
  display: flex;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: .7rem;
  padding: .65rem .9rem;
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
  font-size: .95rem;
  line-height: 1.45;
  color: var(--fg);
  /* ⚠️ A flex row sizes its text to its longest word; a long one breaks rather
     than pushing the card past a phone's width. */
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: border-color var(--dur-quick) var(--ease), background-color var(--dur-quick) var(--ease);
}

section[data-practice-quiz] label:hover { border-color: var(--rule-strong); }

section[data-practice-quiz] label:has(input:checked) {
  background: var(--accent-soft);
  border-color: var(--accent);
}

section[data-practice-quiz] label:has(input:focus-visible) {
  outline: 2px solid var(--focus);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

section[data-practice-quiz] label input {
  flex: none;
  width: 1rem;
  height: 1rem;
  margin: .18rem 0 0;
  accent-color: var(--accent);
}

/* --- after Check: the chosen option's verdict ------------------------------ */

/* ⭐ Right is `--ok` and wrong is `--bad`, on the CHOSEN option only, and each
   says so twice more: the mark in the option (an icon and the word) and the
   sentence under the question. ⛔ The key is never shown — a reader told why
   their choice fails can try again. */
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="correct"] label:has(input:checked) {
  background: var(--ok-soft);
  border-color: var(--ok);
}

section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="wrong"] label:has(input:checked) {
  background: var(--bad-soft);
  border-color: var(--bad);
}

section[data-practice-quiz] [data-practice-part="mark"] {
  display: inline-flex;
  flex: none;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .3rem;
  margin: .12rem 0 0 auto;
  padding-left: .5rem;
  font-size: .8rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  white-space: nowrap;
}

section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="correct"] [data-practice-part="mark"] { color: var(--ok); }
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="wrong"] [data-practice-part="mark"] { color: var(--bad); }

/* ⛔ The sentence behind whatever the reader chose, right or wrong, and it is
   told apart by the WORD in front of it — *Right.* or *Not this one.* — which
   `practice-quiz.js` reads off the markup, by the icon before it, and only
   then by its ground. The exam form's verdict line is the same object. */
section[data-practice-quiz] p[data-practice-part="says"],
section[data-practice-quiz] [data-form-part="review"] p[data-form-part="verdict"] {
  position: relative;
  margin: .75rem 0 0;
  padding: .6rem .85rem;
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
  font-size: .9rem;
  line-height: 1.5;
  color: var(--fg);
}

section[data-practice-quiz] p[data-practice-part="says"][hidden] { display: none; }

section[data-practice-quiz] p[data-practice-part="says"] strong { margin-right: .1rem; }

section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="correct"] p[data-practice-part="says"],
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="correct"] > [data-form-part="review"] p[data-form-part="verdict"] {
  background: var(--ok-soft);
  border-color: var(--ok);
}

section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="wrong"] p[data-practice-part="says"],
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="wrong"] > [data-form-part="review"] p[data-form-part="verdict"] {
  background: var(--bad-soft);
  border-color: var(--bad);
}

/* The two icons, drawn as masks over the verdict's ink so they take its
   colour. ⚠️ Shapes only, no colour written inside them: the palette is the
   only place a colour is written. */
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict] [data-practice-part="mark"]::before,
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict] p[data-practice-part="says"]::before,
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict] > [data-form-part="review"] p[data-form-part="verdict"]::before {
  content: "";
  flex: none;
  width: 1.05rem;
  height: 1.05rem;
  background: currentColor;
}

/* ⚠️ The sentence is running text with inline code in it, so it stays a block
   and the icon is placed in its left padding rather than made a flex item. */
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict] p[data-practice-part="says"],
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict] > [data-form-part="review"] p[data-form-part="verdict"] {
  padding-left: 2.2rem;
}

section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict] p[data-practice-part="says"]::before,
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict] > [data-form-part="review"] p[data-form-part="verdict"]::before {
  position: absolute;
  top: .78rem;
  left: .8rem;
}

section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="correct"] p[data-practice-part="says"]::before,
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="correct"] > [data-form-part="review"] p[data-form-part="verdict"]::before { color: var(--ok); }

section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="wrong"] p[data-practice-part="says"]::before,
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="wrong"] > [data-form-part="review"] p[data-form-part="verdict"]::before { color: var(--bad); }

section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="correct"] [data-practice-part="mark"]::before,
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="correct"] p[data-practice-part="says"]::before,
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="correct"] > [data-form-part="review"] p[data-form-part="verdict"]::before {
  mask: url("data:image/svg+xml;base64,") center / contain no-repeat;
}

section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="wrong"] [data-practice-part="mark"]::before,
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="wrong"] p[data-practice-part="says"]::before,
section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-verdict="wrong"] > [data-form-part="review"] p[data-form-part="verdict"]::before {
  mask: url("data:image/svg+xml;base64,") center / contain no-repeat;
}

/* --- the controls and the score -------------------------------------------- */

section[data-practice-quiz] p[data-practice-part="controls"] {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: .5rem;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: flex-end;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 1rem 0 0;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
}

section[data-practice-quiz] p[data-practice-part="controls"][hidden] { display: none; }

section[data-practice-quiz] button {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  gap: .4rem;
  font: inherit;
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: .95rem;
  padding: .45rem 1rem;
  color: var(--fg);
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
  cursor: pointer;
}

section[data-practice-quiz] button[hidden] { display: none; }

section[data-practice-quiz] button:hover { background: var(--surface-2); }

/* ⭐ The one primary act of a quiz: Check answers (Finish quiz in the exam
   form). An accent ground with the surface's ink — a control, never running
   text. */
section[data-practice-quiz] button[data-practice-part="check"],
section[data-practice-quiz] button[data-form-part="submit"] {
  color: var(--surface);
  background: var(--accent);
  border-color: var(--accent);
}

section[data-practice-quiz] button[data-practice-part="check"]:hover,
section[data-practice-quiz] button[data-form-part="submit"]:hover {
  color: var(--surface);
  background: var(--accent);
  text-decoration: underline;
}

section[data-practice-quiz] button[data-practice-part="reset"] { margin-right: auto; }

/* The score line: "2 of 4 answered correctly." */
section[data-practice-quiz] p[data-practice-part="status"] {
  margin: .75rem 0 0;
  font-size: .9rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
}

section[data-practice-quiz] p[data-practice-part="status"]:empty { display: none; }

@media (max-width: 40rem) {
  section[data-practice-quiz] { padding: 1rem .9rem 1.1rem; }
  section[data-practice-quiz] li[data-practice-question] { padding: .8rem .7rem .85rem; }
  section[data-practice-quiz] label { padding: .6rem .7rem; }
  /* ⚠️ On a phone the mark takes its own line under the option's words, so the
     sentence keeps the width: it sits in the label's bottom padding. */
  section[data-practice-quiz] label { position: relative; }
  section[data-practice-quiz] label:has([data-practice-part="mark"]) { padding-bottom: 1.9rem; }
  section[data-practice-quiz] [data-practice-part="mark"] {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: .55rem;
    left: 2.4rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  section[data-practice-quiz] label { transition: none; }
}

/* A lesson's practice cards: the Practice (n) list, and a placed practice's card.

   ⛔ NO CLASS but the card's `.eyebrow`, the standing rule for a region the
   framework emits: the list is reached by `data-practices` and a card by
   `data-practice-card`, both emitted by `render/page/practices.py` alone; the
   workspace a card opens is `practice-workspace.css`.

   ⛔ NO COLOUR OF ITS OWN. Every value is a palette token. */

/* --- the list ------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ⭐ THE 2026-11 REDESIGN: A CARD THAT OPENS A PRACTICE. It stays compact (the
   user's decision: the practice's material is never inlined into the lesson)
   and reads like the header of the practice it opens: the kind label (an
   `.eyebrow`: *Coding practice*, *Concept check*, and the practice's language
   when it names one), the title, what it practises, its state, and a chevron.
   ⭐ The WHOLE CARD is the link's hit area — the title link's `::after` is
   stretched over it — so there is still one link, one name and one tab stop. */
section[data-practices] ol[data-practices-part="cards"] {
  display: grid;
  /* ⚠️ One column that may be narrower than a card's longest word: a grid
     track is at least its content's min-content by default, and a long
     identifier in "what it practises" pushed the card past a phone's edge. */
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  gap: .85rem;
  margin: 1rem 0 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

section[data-practices] li[data-practice-card] {
  position: relative;
  max-width: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 1rem 3.25rem 1.05rem 1.25rem;
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  box-shadow: var(--card-shadow);
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  transition: border-color var(--dur-quick) var(--ease);
}

section[data-practices] li[data-practice-card]:hover { border-color: var(--rule-strong); }


/* The kind label, its parts parted by a drawn dot. ⛔ Never a middle-dot
   character: the dot is a 3px circle. */
section[data-practices] li[data-practice-card] .eyebrow {
  margin: 0 0 .3rem;
  max-width: none;
  font-size: .72rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: .08em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--accent);
}

section[data-practices] p[data-practices-part="kind"] > span + span { color: var(--muted); }

section[data-practices] p[data-practices-part="kind"] > span + span::before {
  content: "";
  display: inline-block;
  width: 3px;
  height: 3px;
  margin: 0 .55rem;
  vertical-align: middle;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: currentColor;
}

section[data-practices] li[data-practice-card] h3 {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 1.12rem;
  line-height: 1.35;
}

section[data-practices] a[data-practices-part="open"] {
  color: var(--fg);
  text-decoration: none;
}

/* ⭐ The focused link draws its own ring (`test_keyboard`: whatever focus lands on shows one),
   offset so it clears the title; the whole card stays the hit area through `::after`. */
section[data-practices] a[data-practices-part="open"]:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--focus); outline-offset: 3px; }

section[data-practices] a[data-practices-part="open"]::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  border-radius: inherit;
}

section[data-practices] li[data-practice-card]:hover a[data-practices-part="open"] {
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
  text-underline-offset: .2em;
}

/* The open affordance: a chevron at the card's right, which takes the accent
   when the card is pointed at. */
section[data-practices] svg[data-practices-part="chevron"] {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  right: 1rem;
  margin-top: -10px;
  color: var(--muted);
}

section[data-practices] li[data-practice-card]:hover svg[data-practices-part="chevron"] { color: var(--accent); }

/* What it practises: a short bulleted list under the title. */
section[data-practices] ul[data-practices-part="concepts"] {
  margin: .5rem 0 0;
  padding: 0 0 0 1.1rem;
  max-width: none;
  list-style: disc;
}

section[data-practices] ul[data-practices-part="concepts"] li {
  margin: .15rem 0 0;
  padding: 0;
  max-width: none;
  font-size: .88rem;
  line-height: 1.45;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
}

section[data-practices] ul[data-practices-part="concepts"] li::marker { color: var(--muted); }

/* ⭐ The state, a small pill: *Not started* with an empty ring, *Passed* with
   the `--ok` tick. ⛔ A state is told by its WORD first and its icon second,
   and the colour is only the third signal. */
section[data-practices] p[data-practices-part="state"] {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .35rem;
  margin: .65rem 0 0;
  padding: .12rem .55rem .12rem .45rem;
  font-size: .8rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--muted);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-sign);
}

section[data-practices] p[data-practices-part="state"][hidden] { display: none; }

section[data-practices] p[data-practices-part="state"]::before {
  content: "";
  width: .7rem;
  height: .7rem;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  border: 1.5px solid currentColor;
  border-radius: 50%;
}

section[data-practices] li[data-practice-state="passed"] p[data-practices-part="state"] {
  color: var(--ok);
  background: var(--ok-soft);
  border-color: var(--ok);
}

section[data-practices] li[data-practice-state="passed"] p[data-practices-part="state"]::before {
  width: .9rem;
  height: .9rem;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 0;
  background: currentColor;
  mask: url("data:image/svg+xml;base64,") center / contain no-repeat;
}

/* ⭐ A practice written in several languages is ONE card: the sentence naming its editions, each
   marked once it is passed, and a status that says how many are. ⛔ A mark is a word first
   (*passed*), never colour alone. */
section[data-practices] p[data-practices-part="editions"] {
  margin: .45rem 0 0;
  max-width: none;
  font-size: .82rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

section[data-practices] [data-edition-mark] { color: var(--done); font-weight: 700; }

section[data-practices] li[data-practice-state="partial"] p[data-practices-part="state"] {
  color: var(--fg-soft);
}

@media (max-width: 40rem) {
  section[data-practices] li[data-practice-card] { padding: .9rem 2.75rem .95rem 1rem; }
  section[data-practices] svg[data-practices-part="chevron"] { top: 2.1rem; right: .75rem; }
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  section[data-practices] li[data-practice-card] { transition: none; }
}

/* A lesson's practices: the one workspace a card opens in. ⭐ The Practice (n)
   list of cards is `practice-cards.css` (split out in the 2026-11 redesign,
   when the card grew past what one part may hold).

   ⛔ NO CLASS, the standing rule for a region the framework emits: the list is
   reached by `data-practices`, a card by `data-practice-card` and the workspace
   by `data-workspace`, all emitted by `render/page/practices.py` alone.

   ⛔ NO COLOUR OF ITS OWN. Every value is a palette token: a card stands on the
   surface the notes stand on, and the workspace's ground is the page's own.

   ⭐ **THE WORKSPACE IS GEOMETRY, AND NOTHING MOVES.** The open practice's own
   section and panel stay where the page put them; `practice-workspace.js` marks
   the pair `data-workspace-open`, and the rules below lay the section over the
   left of the viewport and the panel over the right, under the workspace's bar.
   ⛔ An `iframe` MOVED TO ANOTHER PARENT RELOADS, and a rule cannot reparent
   anything — so the shape that could throw a reader's work away is not
   reachable from here.

   ⚠️ **`position: fixed` answers to the VIEWPORT only while no ancestor makes a
   containing block for it** — a `transform`, `filter`, `will-change`,
   `perspective` or `contain` above the practice pins it to that box instead.
   ⛔ No authored part of this bundle declares one, and
   `tests/studyforge/render/pageassets/test_bundle.py` reads the bundle for it.

   ⭐ **THE PAGE DOES NOT CHANGE HEIGHT WHEN A PRACTICE OPENS.** Every practice
   is already `hidden` — out of flow — once the script has run, and an open one
   is `fixed`, out of flow too; so the scroll position `practice-workspace.js`
   puts back on Close is one the page can still hold. */

/* --- the workspace -------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ⛔ The page under the workspace does not scroll while it is up: the reader
   scrolls the statement and the panel, each inside itself. */
html[data-workspace-open]:not([data-workspace-quiz]) { overflow: hidden; }

/* ⭐ The shell is the ground and the bar. ⛔ Above BOTH of the page's other
   raised layers — the skip link at 2 and the narration transport at 1 — or the
   workspace is covered by the bar it was meant to cover. */
div[data-workspace] {
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0;
  z-index: 3;
  background: var(--bg);
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
}

div[data-workspace] div[data-workspace-part="bar"] {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .75rem;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  height: 3.25rem;
  padding: 0 1rem;
  background: var(--panel);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
}

/* ⚠️ A long title gives way to the three buttons rather than pushing them off
   the bar: it is one line, cut with an ellipsis. */
div[data-workspace] h2[data-workspace-part="title"] {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-width: 0;
  margin: 0;
  font-size: .95rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  white-space: nowrap;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  color: var(--fg);
}

div[data-workspace] p[data-workspace-part="acts"] {
  display: flex;
  gap: .4rem;
  margin: 0;
  max-width: none;
}

/* ⭐ The 2026-11 redesign: the bar's acts are quiet hairline controls, the
   card language of the lesson, so the practice's title leads the bar. */
div[data-workspace] button {
  font: inherit;
  font-weight: 600;
  font-size: .85rem;
  padding: .32rem .75rem;
  color: var(--fg);
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
  box-shadow: var(--card-shadow);
  cursor: pointer;
}

div[data-workspace] button:hover { border-color: var(--rule-strong); background: var(--surface-2); }

/* ⭐ The statement on the left, always visible and scrolled inside itself; the
   panel on the right. ⛔ Above the shell, which is their ground. */
section[data-workspace-open] {
  position: fixed;
  top: 3.25rem;
  bottom: 0;
  z-index: 4;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  margin: 0;
  overflow: auto;
}

section[data-kind][data-workspace-open] {
  left: 0;
  width: 42%;
  padding: 1rem 1.25rem 2rem;
  background: var(--bg);
  border-right: 1px solid var(--rule);
}

section:is([data-practice], [data-practice-quiz])[data-workspace-open] {
  left: 42%;
  right: 0;
  padding: 1rem 1.25rem 1.25rem;
  border-top: 0;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}

/* ⛔ **A language-tagged practice stays a flex column when it opens.** The reading modes' generated
   rules show a tagged section the reader is linked to with `display: block`
   (a section carrying `data-lang` and `data-linked` under a reading mode), which outweighs the rule above and
   turned the open panel into a block: the editor kept its natural height, about eight lines, with
   the rest of the pane empty, and no divider could change it. Repeating the attribute raises this
   rule over those without naming a mode or a language. A quiz is excluded: it is a page in flow. */
html[data-workspace-open]:not([data-workspace-quiz])
  section:is([data-practice], [data-practice-quiz])[data-workspace-open][data-workspace-open][data-workspace-open] {
  display: flex;
}

/* ⭐ The editor slot takes the room: the note, the controls, the status and the
   output keep the size they had, and what a reader needs more of is the CODE.
   ⚠️ `min-height` holds a usable editor when the output grows. */
section[data-practice][data-workspace-open] div[data-practice-part="editor"] {
  flex: 1 0 18rem;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}

section[data-practice][data-workspace-open] div[data-practice-frame] {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-height: 0;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}

section[data-practice][data-workspace-open] div[data-practice-part="editor"] iframe {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  height: 100%;
  min-height: 16rem;
}

/* ⭐ **The editor is the surface the reader types in, so its frame fills its box.** The box
   was drawn for the sentence that says the editor is not running: a dashed border, padding and
   a gap below, which together held the frame about a tenth of the pane away from the edges of the
   box that was already most of it. ⚠️ Once the workspace is open and a frame is showing, the box
   is only a column for the tabs and the frame: no border, no padding, no margin, and the frame
   takes everything the tabs leave, down to the 16rem a usable editor needs: in a short window
   it runs past the pane's foot and the pane scrolls, as it did before. */
html[data-workspace-open] section[data-practice][data-workspace-open]
  div[data-practice-part="editor"] {
  padding: 0;
  margin: 0;
  border: 0;
  background: none;
}

html[data-workspace-open] section[data-practice][data-workspace-open]
  div[data-practice-part="editor"] {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
  grid-template-rows: auto minmax(16rem, 1fr);
  align-items: center;
  column-gap: .75rem;
}

/* ⭐ **One row above the frame, not two**: the tabs on the left and the link that opens the
   editor in a tab of its own on the right, so the link stays visible without a line to itself.
   ⚠️ The link keeps its column when the tablist is hidden, so it never sits over the frame. */
html[data-workspace-open] section[data-practice][data-workspace-open]
  div[data-practice-part="editor"] div[data-practice-part="tabs"] {
  grid-column: 1;
  grid-row: 1;
  margin: 0 0 .25rem;
}

html[data-workspace-open] section[data-practice][data-workspace-open]
  div[data-practice-part="editor"] a[data-practice-popout] {
  grid-column: 2;
  grid-row: 1;
  margin: 0 0 .25rem;
  white-space: nowrap;
}

html[data-workspace-open] section[data-practice][data-workspace-open]
  div[data-practice-part="editor"] > p {
  grid-column: 1 / -1;
  grid-row: 1;
}

html[data-workspace-open] section[data-practice][data-workspace-open]
  div[data-practice-part="editor"] div[data-practice-frame] {
  grid-column: 1 / -1;
  grid-row: 2;
  align-self: stretch;
}

/* ⭐ The two notes above the editor take a line each at a small size and little room, so the
   frame under them keeps the height. */
html[data-workspace-open] section[data-practice][data-workspace-open] p[data-practice-part="grader"],
html[data-workspace-open] section[data-practice][data-workspace-open] p[data-practice-part="file"] {
  margin: 0 0 .25rem;
  font-size: .85rem;
  line-height: 1.35;
}

/* ⭐ **A QUIZ OPENS AS A PAGE THAT SCROLLS AS ONE.** It has no editor to set beside its intro, and a
   fixed box of its own with its own scrollbar, drawn in a narrow column in the middle of a wide
   screen, is a page inside a page. So a quiz's intro and questions are LEFT IN THE PAGE'S FLOW, at
   the page's own reading width: `practice-workspace.js` marks everything else on the page
   `data-workspace-away` while the quiz is up, the bar stays fixed over the top, and the one
   scrollbar is the window's, at the edge of the screen. ⛔ Nothing is moved: the sections are
   simply no longer fixed. ⚠️ These selectors outweigh the editor layout above and the phone's
   stacked halves below, so a quiz keeps its one column at every width. */
html[data-workspace-quiz] section[data-kind][data-workspace-open],
html[data-workspace-quiz] section[data-practice-quiz][data-workspace-open] {
  position: static;
  display: block;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  width: auto;
  max-width: 52rem;
  max-height: none;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
  overflow: visible;
  border: 0;
  background: var(--bg);
}

html[data-workspace-quiz] section[data-kind][data-workspace-open] { padding-bottom: .5rem; }

html[data-workspace-quiz] div[data-workspace] {
  bottom: auto;
  height: 3.25rem;
}

html[data-workspace-quiz] body { padding-top: 3.25rem; }
html[data-workspace-quiz] [data-workspace-away] { display: none; }

/* ⭐ At phone width the workspace STACKS: the statement over the editor, each
   scrolled inside itself. */
@media (max-width: 40rem) {
  div[data-workspace] div[data-workspace-part="bar"] { padding: 0 .6rem; gap: .4rem; }
  div[data-workspace] button { padding: .3rem .55rem; font-size: .85rem; }

  section[data-kind][data-workspace-open] {
    width: auto;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 55%;
    padding: .75rem 1rem 1rem;
    border-right: 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  section:is([data-practice], [data-practice-quiz])[data-workspace-open] {
    top: 45%;
    left: 0;
    padding: .75rem 1rem 1rem;
  }
}


/* ⭐ The language switch at the top of an edition's panel: the practice's editions, the open one
   told apart by ink and weight as well as by its ground. `hidden` is honoured over `display`. */
section[data-practice] p[data-practice-part="editions"] {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: .4rem;
  margin: 0 0 .6rem;
}

section[data-practice] p[data-practice-part="editions"][hidden] { display: none; }

section[data-practice] button[data-edition-switch][aria-pressed="true"] {
  color: var(--practice);
  background: var(--practice-soft);
  border-color: var(--practice);
}

/* The practice workspace's panes: the description beside the editor resized by
   a divider and closed, and the report under the editor resized and collapsed.

   ⭐ In a code practice's workspace the reader
   drags the divider between the description and the editor to widen or narrow
   the description, and closes it so the editor takes the whole width; drags the
   divider above the report to give the code more height, and collapses the
   report to a slim bar at the bottom that still shows the last verdict.
   Closing both is the focus mode: there is no separate maximise.

   ⛔ NO CLASS AND NO COLOUR OF ITS OWN, the standing rules of
   `practice-workspace.css`, which this file refines and therefore follows in
   `bundle.STYLE_PARTS`. The two widths are custom properties `practice-panes.js`
   sets on the root (`--workspace-left`, `--workspace-report`); with no script,
   none of this applies, because every control here ships `hidden`.

   ⚠️ **The left-hand divider stands in a GUTTER of the shell**, between the two
   fixed sections rather than over either: the sections are raised above the
   shell, so a divider painted over one of them would be covered by it.

   ⛔ **While a divider is dragged no frame takes the pointer.** The editor is
   an `iframe`, and a pointer that crosses into one is the frame's, not the
   page's: the drag would stop dead over the code. The root carries
   `data-workspace-dragging` for exactly as long as the drag, and every frame
   ignores the pointer under it. */

/* --- the left side: the divider, the toggle and the edge ------------------ */

/* ⭐ The two sides meet at the reader's width, and the gutter between them is
   the divider's. ⚠️ Only beside the editor and only above phone width: a quiz
   is one column, and at phone width the workspace stacks. */
@media not all and (max-width: 40rem) {
  html:not([data-workspace-quiz]) section[data-kind][data-workspace-open] {
    width: var(--workspace-left, 42%);
  }

  html:not([data-workspace-quiz]) section:is([data-practice], [data-practice-quiz])[data-workspace-open] {
    left: calc(var(--workspace-left, 42%) + .75rem);
  }

  /* ⭐ Closed, the description is gone and the editor takes the width, less
     the slim edge that opens it again. */
  html[data-workspace-statement="closed"]:not([data-workspace-quiz])
    section[data-kind][data-workspace-open] {
    display: none;
  }

  html[data-workspace-statement="closed"]:not([data-workspace-quiz])
    section:is([data-practice], [data-practice-quiz])[data-workspace-open] {
    left: 2.25rem;
  }
}

div[data-workspace] div[data-workspace-part="divider"] {
  position: fixed;
  top: 3.25rem;
  bottom: 0;
  left: var(--workspace-left, 42%);
  width: .75rem;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  cursor: col-resize;
  touch-action: none;
  background: var(--panel);
  border-left: 1px solid var(--rule);
  border-right: 1px solid var(--rule);
}

/* ⭐ The grip: a rule down the middle of the gutter, which takes the accent
   while the divider is pointed at, dragged or focused. */
div[data-workspace] div[data-workspace-part="divider"]::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  width: 2px;
  height: 2.5rem;
  margin: -1.25rem 0 0 -1px;
  border-radius: 1px;
  background: var(--rule-strong);
}

/* ⭐ The horizontal divider above the report, the same gutter turned. */
section[data-practice] div[data-practice-part="report-divider"] {
  position: relative;
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  height: .75rem;
  margin: .35rem 0 0;
  cursor: row-resize;
  touch-action: none;
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
}

section[data-practice] div[data-practice-part="report-divider"]::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  width: 2.5rem;
  height: 2px;
  margin: -1px 0 0 -1.25rem;
  border-radius: 1px;
  background: var(--rule-strong);
}

:is(div[data-workspace-part="divider"], div[data-practice-part="report-divider"]):hover::after,
:is(div[data-workspace-part="divider"], div[data-practice-part="report-divider"]):focus-visible::after,
html[data-workspace-dragging] :is(div[data-workspace-part="divider"], div[data-practice-part="report-divider"])::after {
  background: var(--focus);
}

/* ⛔ The focus ring is drawn INSIDE the divider: the sections beside the left
   one stand above it and would cover a ring drawn outside. The token is the
   theme's own `--focus`, light and dark. */
:is(div[data-workspace-part="divider"], div[data-practice-part="report-divider"]):focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--focus);
  outline-offset: -2px;
}

/* ⛔ The drag's own rule, argued at the top of this file. */
html[data-workspace-dragging] iframe { pointer-events: none; }
html[data-workspace-dragging],
html[data-workspace-dragging] * { user-select: none; }
html[data-workspace-dragging="x"] * { cursor: col-resize; }
html[data-workspace-dragging="y"] * { cursor: row-resize; }

/* ⭐ The toggle in the bar sits before the title, where the pane it closes is. */
div[data-workspace] button[data-workspace-act="statement"] { flex: 0 0 auto; }

/* ⭐ **The edge that opens the description again**: a slim column down the
   left of the workspace, labelled in words that run down it. */
div[data-workspace] button[data-workspace-act="reopen"] {
  position: fixed;
  top: 3.25rem;
  bottom: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 2.25rem;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .5rem;
  padding: .75rem 0;
  border-width: 0 1px 0 0;
  border-radius: 0;
  background: var(--panel);
}

div[data-workspace] button[data-workspace-act="reopen"] span[data-workspace-part="edge-word"] {
  writing-mode: vertical-rl;
  font-size: .85rem;
}

/* --- the right side: the report under the editor -------------------------- */

/* ⭐ In the workspace the report is a column of its own at the foot of the
   panel: its bar, then its body, which scrolls inside itself. Unsized, it is
   as tall as what it holds, up to under half the panel; once the reader has
   dragged it, it is the height they chose. */
section[data-practice][data-workspace-open] div[data-practice-part="report"] {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  min-height: 0;
  max-height: 45%;
}

html[data-workspace-report-sized] section[data-practice][data-workspace-open]
  div[data-practice-part="report"] {
  height: var(--workspace-report, auto);
  max-height: none;
}

section[data-practice][data-workspace-open] div[data-practice-part="report-body"] {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-height: 0;
  overflow: auto;
}

section[data-practice][data-workspace-open] pre[data-practice-part="output"] { max-height: none; }

/* ⭐ **Until a Run or Submit reports, the report is only its strip**: its body (the status, the
   cases and the output) and the divider above it show once the panel is marked reported
   (`practice-panes.js`), so the editor fills the pane on open. */
section[data-practice][data-workspace-open]:not([data-practice-reported])
  :is(div[data-practice-part="report-body"], div[data-practice-part="report-divider"]) {
  display: none;
}

/* ⭐ The editor gives way to a taller report down to a floor, and takes back
   every pixel the report gives up: the point of both dividers is the code.
   ⚠️ `practice-panes.js` reads this `min-height` as the clamp's floor, so the
   two cannot disagree. */
section[data-practice][data-workspace-open] div[data-practice-part="editor"] {
  flex: 1 1 10rem;
  min-height: 10rem;
}

section[data-practice][data-workspace-open] div[data-practice-part="editor"] iframe {
  min-height: 6rem;
}

/* ⭐ Collapsed, the report is its bar alone, at the foot of the workspace, and
   the editor takes the height. */
html section[data-practice][data-workspace-open][data-practice-report="closed"]
  div[data-practice-part="report"] {
  height: auto;
  max-height: none;
}

/* ⭐ The bar: the whole width, one line, the last verdict at a glance. */
section[data-practice] button[data-practice-part="report-bar"] {
  display: flex;
  gap: .75rem;
  align-items: baseline;
  width: 100%;
  margin: .35rem 0 0;
  padding: .3rem .75rem;
  text-align: left;
  font-size: .85rem;
}

section[data-practice] button[data-practice-part="report-bar"] span[data-practice-part="glance"] {
  font-weight: 400;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
  white-space: nowrap;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

/* A lesson's code examples: one collapsed entry per example, opened in place.

   ⛔ NO CLASS: the list is reached by `data-code-examples`, which
   `render/page/code.py` is the single emitter of, and each entry by
   `data-code-example`. ⭐ An entry is a real `<details>`, so it opens, closes
   and takes keyboard focus with no script at all — which is what a built page,
   with no editor, still needs.

   ⛔ NO COLOUR AND NO MEASURE OF ITS OWN. Every value is a palette token, and
   the editor's box is the practice panel's measure — the same workbench, not a
   second design. ⭐ The entry spans the page's width, because an editor at the
   prose measure is too narrow to read code in. */

div[data-code-examples] {
  margin: .5rem 0 1rem;
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
}

div[data-code-examples] details[data-code-example] {
  margin: 0;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
}

div[data-code-examples] details[data-code-example]:last-child {
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
}

div[data-code-examples] summary {
  padding: .45rem 0;
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  font-variant-ligatures: none;
  font-feature-settings: "liga" 0, "calt" 0;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--fg);
  cursor: pointer;
}

div[data-code-examples] details[open] {
  padding: 0 0 .75rem;
}

div[data-code-examples] ul,
div[data-code-examples] ol {
  margin: 0 0 .5rem;
}

div[data-code-examples] p {
  margin: 0 0 .5rem;
  font-size: .95rem;
  color: var(--fg-soft);
}

div[data-code-examples] div[data-code-part="tabs"] {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: .4rem;
  margin: 0 0 .5rem;
}

/* ⭐ The editor's windows, one visible at a time — never a split pane. ⚠️ A
   box of its own: an editor given no height is 150 pixels tall. */
div[data-code-examples] div[data-code-part="editor"] {
  margin: 0 0 .5rem;
}

div[data-code-examples] div[data-code-part="editor"] iframe {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: 30rem;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
  background: var(--surface-2);
}

div[data-code-examples] p[data-code-part="controls"] {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: .5rem;
  align-items: center;
}

div[data-code-examples] button {
  font: inherit;
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: .95rem;
  padding: .4rem .95rem;
  color: var(--fg);
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
  cursor: pointer;
}

div[data-code-examples] button:hover:not([disabled]) { background: var(--surface-2); }

/* ⛔ The selected tab is told apart by INK AND WEIGHT as well as its ground. */
div[data-code-examples] button[role="tab"][aria-selected="true"] {
  color: var(--practice);
  background: var(--practice-soft);
  border-color: var(--practice);
}

div[data-code-examples] button[disabled] {
  color: var(--muted);
  cursor: default;
  opacity: .65;
}

div[data-code-examples] p[data-code-part="status"] {
  font-size: .9rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

/* ⛔ The test's own output scrolls inside its own box, never the page. */
div[data-code-examples] pre[data-code-part="output"] {
  margin: .6rem 0 0;
  max-height: 20rem;
  overflow: auto;
  padding: .6rem .75rem;
  background: var(--code-bg);
  color: var(--code-fg);
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  font-variant-ligatures: none;
  font-feature-settings: "liga" 0, "calt" 0;
  font-size: .85rem;
  line-height: 1.5;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
}

/* Syntax highlighting for the lesson's code blocks.

   Prism (vendored, `prism.js`) tokenises in the browser and writes
   `<span class="token …">` inside the `<code>` the page already shipped, so
   NO generated page changes and the archive stays clean text. With JS off,
   every block falls back to the single `--code-fg` it had before.

   ⛔ A LANGUAGE WITH NO PRISM GRAMMAR RENDERS AS THE DECLARED `plain`
   FALLBACK, and so is `text` left uncoloured, whose grammar is empty by
   design. A corpus in a language nobody vendored a grammar for must render,
   not fail, and its caption says it is plain text. ⚠️ The framework knows no
   corpus's languages (R1): what is highlightable is the `Languages:` line of
   the vendored bundle's header, checked against the grammars it carries.

   ⛔ ONE ELEMENT OFTEN CARRIES SEVERAL TOKEN CLASSES, and they can want
   different colours: Java's `@Override` is `annotation punctuation`,
   JavaScript's backtick is `template-punctuation string`, Kotlin's `"…"` is
   `string-literal singleline`. Every selector here has the same specificity,
   so **the last group to match wins** -- which makes the ORDER OF THE GROUPS
   BELOW the actual mapping, not decoration. It runs from weakest to
   strongest: container, punctuation, type, function, number, string,
   keyword, comment, and finally interpolation in the body colour. Moving a
   group changes what colour a combined token takes;
   `tests/studyforge/render/pageassets/test_highlight.py` runs the real
   grammars and checks the winner for every combination they emit.

   ⚠️ Kotlin's `singleline` and `multiline` are STRING-LITERAL variants, not
   comment variants. Filing them under comment italicised every string in one
   language and the tests still passed; only a screenshot caught it, which is
   how this rule came to be written down. */

/* Containers Prism nests other tokens inside. They must not paint, or they
   would override the child that carries the meaning. Weakest, deliberately.

   ⚠️ Gherkin's `feature` and `scenario` wrap a keyword and a title, and its
   two table halves wrap their cells, so all four are containers of exactly
   that kind. `important` is the title text itself, which reads as prose and
   not as a painted token. `script` and `language-javascript` wrap the
   JavaScript inside an HTML `<script>` element -- the same shape as
   `language-regex`, which was already here. */
figure.code .token.expression,
figure.code .token.parameter,
figure.code .token.decorator,
figure.code .token.language-regex,
figure.code .token.language-javascript,
figure.code .token.script,
figure.code .token.feature,
figure.code .token.scenario,
figure.code .token.important,
figure.code .token.table-head,
figure.code .token.table-body,
figure.code .token.code-block,
figure.code .token.bold,
figure.code .token.content,
figure.code .token.title { color: inherit; }

/* ⚠️ Markdown's `bold` and `content` wrap the words a reader should see as
   prose, `code-block` wraps a fenced block whose own grammar colours it, and
   `title` wraps a heading line: all four are containers of that kind. */

/* ⛔ A prolog, a doctype and a CDATA marker are STRUCTURAL NOISE, and they
   are filed here rather than under comment on purpose: Prism's own themes
   colour them as comments, and this file's standing rule is that nothing
   takes the comment colour and its italics unless it IS a comment. */
figure.code .token.punctuation,
figure.code .token.operator,
figure.code .token.template-punctuation,
figure.code .token.interpolation-punctuation,
figure.code .token.regex-delimiter,
figure.code .token.regex-flags,
figure.code .token.prolog,
figure.code .token.doctype,
figure.code .token.cdata,
figure.code .token.shebang,
figure.code .token.list { color: var(--tok-punct); }

/* ⭐ The names a document DECLARES read as types: an element name, an
   attribute name, a JSON property, a properties key, a Gherkin placeholder
   or table heading. Their VALUES are filed under string, below. */
figure.code .token.class-name,
figure.code .token.builtin,
figure.code .token.annotation,
figure.code .token.generics,
figure.code .token.namespace,
figure.code .token.tag,
figure.code .token.attr-name,
figure.code .token.doctype-tag,
figure.code .token.name,
figure.code .token.property,
figure.code .token.key,
figure.code .token.code-language,
figure.code .token.constant,
figure.code .token.variable { color: var(--tok-type); }

figure.code .token.function,
figure.code .token.function-variable { color: var(--tok-function); }

figure.code .token.number { color: var(--tok-number); }

figure.code .token.string,
figure.code .token.string-literal,
figure.code .token.string-interpolation,
figure.code .token.triple-quoted-string,
figure.code .token.template-string,
figure.code .token.singleline,
figure.code .token.multiline,
figure.code .token.char,
figure.code .token.regex,
figure.code .token.regex-source,
figure.code .token.attr-value,
figure.code .token.value,
figure.code .token.entity,
figure.code .token.url,
figure.code .token.code { color: var(--tok-string); }

/* ⭐ A Gherkin step keyword -- Given, When, Then -- is Prism's `atrule`, and
   it is a keyword in every sense the reader cares about. */
figure.code .token.keyword,
figure.code .token.boolean,
figure.code .token.import,
figure.code .token.atrule { color: var(--tok-keyword); font-weight: 600; }

figure.code .token.comment { color: var(--tok-comment); font-style: italic; }

/* An interpolated expression is code, not string: `"Hi $n"` reads with `$n`
   in the body colour, the way an IDE shows it, so the reader can see what is
   substituted. It is a CHILD of the string element, so it wins on being the
   inner element rather than on order. */
figure.code .token.interpolation { color: var(--code-fg); font-style: normal; }

/* The narration transport, and the highlight that tracks what is being spoken.

   ⭐ THE SCOPE IS THE ONE REGION `chrome.css` DEFERRED. It says of itself:
   "`footer` is absent … The narration player is the only `<footer>` a page
   carries, it is the narration player's, and `--player-height`, `--panel` and
   `--shadow` are defined and waiting for it." ⛔ This is that part. Five palette
   tokens were `UNPAINTED` in `tests/visual/palette.py`'s ledger with the player
   named against each; painting them is what forces the ledger to say which
   ground each one's contrast is taken against.

   ⛔ NOT ONE COLOUR AND NOT ONE MEASURE IS DEFINED HERE. Every value is a
   `palette.css` token, so the transport follows the reader's theme and
   `test_palette` fails a raw hex.

   ⛔ NOT ONE CLASS NAME IS TARGETED HERE EITHER, for the reason `chrome.css`
   gives: every hook is an element, an id from `templates/player.html`, or a
   `data-*` attribute, so nothing here joins the published class contract and no
   page has to be re-rendered for a style change.

   ⚠️ `data-speaking` is this part's and `narration.js`'s, and it is deliberately
   NOT in `pageassets.SURFACE_HOOKS`. `data-marked` is published because
   `chrome.css` paints a state `read-mark.js` writes — two parts, two ends of
   one spelling. Here both ends are one feature's, and publishing a hook nobody else
   may reach would oblige a contract that has no second side.

   ⚠️ Loaded after `code-highlight.css` and before the vendored `plyr.css`: the
   highlight refines the inside of a code block, which the highlighter has just
   painted, and at equal specificity the last rule wins. The vendored player
   theme stays last so a video control keeps its own look. */

/* --- the transport ------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ⛔ STICKY, NOT FIXED, and that is the reason there is no rule on `body` here.
   A fixed footer overlaps the last paragraph of every unit and has to be paid
   for with a `padding-bottom` on `body` — a second rule answering the question
   `chrome.css` already answers once, on one element. The footer is
   the last child of `body`, so sticking it to the bottom pins it while the
   reader scrolls and lets the page end underneath it.

   ⚠️ It ships `hidden` and `narration.js` unhides it, so every rule here is for
   a transport a reader can actually use. */

footer#player {
  position: sticky;
  bottom: 0;
  z-index: 1;
  min-height: var(--player-height);
  box-sizing: border-box;
  margin-top: 2.5rem;
  padding: 0 0 .55rem;
  background: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow);
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: .92rem;
  color: var(--fg);
}

/* ⭐ THE WHOLE WIDTH OF THE CONTENT: beside the rail the transport
   spans every column right of it — the reading column and the aside — so the
   bar is as wide as the page's content. ⚠️ Only its ground widens: the controls
   below are flex items at their own size and never grow with it. Below the
   width threshold `body` is not a grid, so this line says nothing there. */
body:has(nav[aria-label="Containers"]) > footer#player { grid-column: 2 / -1; }

/* ⭐ ONE ROW: the transport was 140px tall on a laptop and 188px
   on a phone, a fifth of the screen held over the text for the whole visit. */
footer#player > div:last-of-type {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .4rem .6rem;
  padding: .55rem .75rem 0;
}

/* The keyboard sentence is for keyboard readers, so it shows while focus is in
   the transport and never on a touch screen, where it cannot apply (N3). */
footer#player p {
  display: none;
  flex-basis: 100%;
  margin: 0;
  font-size: .82rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

@media (hover: hover) {
  footer#player:focus-within p { display: block; }
}

/* --- progress through the unit ------------------------------------------- */

/* ⛔ The width comes from `--progress`, which `narration.js` sets and nothing
   else reads. The fallback is `0%`: a page whose script never ran shows an
   empty bar rather than a full one. */

#track {
  height: 3px;
  background: var(--surface-2);
  overflow: hidden;
}

#fill {
  width: var(--progress, 0%);
  height: 100%;
  background: var(--hl-bar);
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  #fill {
    transition: width var(--dur-fill) var(--ease);
  }
}

/* --- the controls -------------------------------------------------------- */

footer#player button,
footer#player select {
  font: inherit;
  color: var(--fg);
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
  padding: .3rem .65rem;
  cursor: pointer;
}

footer#player button:hover:enabled,
footer#player select:hover:enabled {
  background: var(--surface-2);
}

/* ⛔ A disabled control is dimmed AND says so through the attribute, never
   through colour alone. */
footer#player button:disabled,
footer#player select:disabled {
  opacity: .55;
  cursor: not-allowed;
}

/* ⭐ Play is the primary control: ink on the button's ground, first in weight.
   Previous and next are glyph buttons whose names are their `aria-label`s. */
footer#player #play {
  order: -1;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--bg);
  background: var(--fg);
  border-color: var(--fg);
}

footer#player #play:hover:enabled { background: var(--fg-soft); }

#previous,
#next {
  min-width: 2.25rem;
  font-weight: 700;
}

/* --- what is playing, and what is wrong ---------------------------------- */

#where {
  color: var(--muted);
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
  max-width: 22rem;
}

#where,
#counter { display: inline-block; vertical-align: bottom; }

/* ⛔ No glyph joins the two (the middle-dot tell, N4): a gap does. */
#where:not(:empty) + #counter { margin-left: .6rem; }

/* On a phone the transport keeps to one row: the section's title goes, the
   passage count stays. */
@media (max-width: 40rem) {
  #where { display: none; }
  #where:not(:empty) + #counter { margin-left: 0; }
  footer#player > div:last-of-type { gap: .4rem; padding-left: .5rem; padding-right: .5rem; }
  footer#player button,
  footer#player select { padding: .3rem .5rem; }
  footer#player select { width: 4.25rem; }
}

#counter {
  color: var(--muted);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}

/* ⛔ Every sentence in here is markup (`templates/player.html`) and the script
   only unhides one; the region itself is hidden when there is nothing to say. */
#status {
  flex-basis: 100%;
  color: var(--fg);
  font-weight: 700;
}

/* --- the promise the record made and the disk did not keep ---------------- */

/* ⛔ THE ONE NOTICE THIS PART PAINTS, AND IT IS NOT ON EVERY PAGE. A corpus that
   was never narrated is COMPLETE rather than short (spec §7 C5, §11.0), so it
   carries nothing at all; this region exists only when the narration record
   PROMISED a clip that is not on disk, so that case never renders identically
   to never having been narrated.

   ⚠️ It is `chrome.css`'s eighth region and is DEFERRED there for the reason
   `footer#player` is: both are the transport's, both are painted here, and that
   file still reaches none of these tokens.

   ⭐ It takes the transport's own ground rather than the page's, because it is
   the transport speaking — and because `--fg` and `--accent` are already
   measured against `--panel` by the footer above. ⛔ NOT hidden: the footer
   ships `hidden` and needs a script to come back, and a reader with scripting
   off must still be told that audio this unit was promised is missing. */

section[data-section="narration-gap"] {
  margin-top: 2.5rem;
  padding: .75rem 1rem;
  background: var(--panel);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: .92rem;
  color: var(--fg);
}

/* ⛔ No coloured stripe down its edge (the brief's tell, N7): a feint rule
   above and the transport's own ground say it is the transport speaking. */
section[data-section="narration-gap"] h2 {
  margin: 0 0 .35rem;
  font-size: 1rem;
  color: var(--fg);
}

section[data-section="narration-gap"] p {
  margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
  color: var(--fg);
}

/* --- the highlight ------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ⛔ Speech-unit granularity: the lit passage is the passage whose clip is
   loaded. ⛔ NOTHING IS LIT BEFORE THE READER STARTS NARRATION:
   `narration.js` sets `data-speaking` only once a passage is loaded by a
   press. ⭐ PAUSED it is a short highlighter mark in the margin beside the
   passage, not a wash: a paused page must not look as though
   something is selected. The wash — the highlighter swiped across the line —
   appears only while narration is playing, which the play button's own face
   says. Both are drawn with `box-shadow` so lighting a passage never moves it. */

[data-speaking] {
  box-shadow: -.8rem 0 0 -.55rem var(--hl-bar);
}

:root:has(#play [data-state="playing"]:not([hidden])) [data-speaking] {
  background: var(--hl-bg);
  color: var(--hl-fg);
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 .3rem var(--hl-bg);
  border-radius: var(--radius-tight);
}

/* ⚠️ A code block inside a lit passage keeps its own ground AND its own text
   colours — the seven syntax tokens were measured against `--code-bg`. It takes
   a wash laid over the top instead, as a `background-image`, so the ground the
   numbers were taken against stays underneath. */
[data-speaking] pre,
[data-speaking] code {
  background-image: linear-gradient(var(--hl-code), var(--hl-code));
}

[data-speaking] pre code {
  background-image: none;
}

