/* Plyr, wearing this page's palette.

   Loaded AFTER the vendored `plyr.css`, never forking it — the standing rule
   for a view that needs something different. Plyr exposes its own custom
   properties, so this is a handful of token assignments rather than a
   restyle, and re-vendoring Plyr does not strand it.

   ⚠️ `#000` and `#fff` here are the two raw colours the palette rule allows,
   and they are not theme colours: black behind a video is the absence of
   picture, and a control drawn on it stays white in both themes because the
   thing underneath it does. */

figure.video .plyr {
  --plyr-color-main: var(--accent);
  --plyr-video-background: #000;
  --plyr-video-control-color: #fff;
  --plyr-menu-background: var(--surface);
  --plyr-menu-color: var(--fg);
  --plyr-menu-border-color: var(--rule);
  --plyr-font-family: var(--font-ui);
  --plyr-font-size-small: .8rem;
  --plyr-font-size-base: .875rem;
  --plyr-range-thumb-background: #fff;
  --plyr-tooltip-background: var(--surface);
  --plyr-tooltip-color: var(--fg);
  border-radius: 10px;
  overflow: hidden;
}

/* Black behind the deck, so a poster that has not arrived reads as a player
   and not as a hole — and it must hold with scripting off, where Plyr never
   runs and the figure is a bare `<video>`. Plyr must not round over it. */
figure.video { background: #000; }
figure.video .plyr__poster { background-color: #000; }

/* The one visible-focus ring the rest of the page uses, so a keyboard reader
   is not handed a second convention inside the player. */
figure.video .plyr__control:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--focus);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

/* ⛔ FULLSCREEN MUST UNDO `max-height: 70vh`.

   `reading.css` caps the deck at 70vh so a video cannot dominate the reading
   column. In fullscreen, `vh` resolves against the FULLSCREEN viewport, so
   the same rule caps the video at 70% of the screen and leaves a black band
   across the bottom third. Plyr sets `height: 100%` but never touches
   `max-height`, so the cap survives and wins.

   ⛔ THERE ARE THREE DIFFERENT HOOKS AND THEY ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE.
   Measured against Plyr 3.8.4's own stylesheet and bundle:

     1. NATIVE, the path a reader actually takes — Plyr fullscreens the
        `.plyr` CONTAINER and styles it `.plyr:fullscreen`. There is no
        "active" class: the bundle only ever sets `fullscreen-enabled` and
        `fullscreen-fallback`. ⚠️ Because the container goes fullscreen and
        not the media element, `video:fullscreen` DOES NOT MATCH here.
     2. FALLBACK, when the browser has no fullscreen API — class-based,
        `.plyr--fullscreen-fallback`.
     3. NO JS AT ALL — the native controls fullscreen the `<video>` itself,
        which is the only case `video:fullscreen` matches.

   ⚠️ `.plyr--fullscreen-active` does not exist, and simulating the FALLBACK
   class verifies only the one path that already works. Check a selector
   against the vendored stylesheet, not against memory.

   Separate rules, never one list: an unknown selector invalidates the WHOLE
   list, so the class-based hooks must not share a list with the pseudo-class
   ones. `object-fit: contain` so a video whose aspect ratio differs from the
   screen is letterboxed rather than stretched. */
figure.video .plyr:fullscreen video {
  max-height: none; height: 100%; width: 100%;
  object-fit: contain; border: 0; border-radius: 0;
}

figure.video .plyr:-webkit-full-screen video {
  max-height: none; height: 100%; width: 100%;
  object-fit: contain; border: 0; border-radius: 0;
}

figure.video .plyr--fullscreen-fallback { border-radius: 0; }

figure.video .plyr--fullscreen-fallback video {
  max-height: none; height: 100%; width: 100%;
  object-fit: contain; border: 0; border-radius: 0;
}

figure.video video:fullscreen {
  max-height: none; height: 100%; width: 100%;
  object-fit: contain; border: 0; border-radius: 0;
}

figure.video video:-webkit-full-screen {
  max-height: none; height: 100%; width: 100%;
  object-fit: contain; border: 0; border-radius: 0;
}
